WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.730
- Good morning, we wanna
get this session started,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.130
and thank you for being here.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.550
So, my name is Eve Rice and on behalf

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.610
of the 50th anniversary
Planning Committee,

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.310
I want to welcome you back
to Yale and to New Haven.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.230
For the past two years,

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.240
it has been my great privilege to chair

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.376
the committee planning for
this anniversary celebration.

00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:36.209
(audience cheers)

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.360
To be sure, this weekend
has been a long time coming,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.110
but we're thrilled that it's here.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.420
I wanna pause for a moment
before we begin the main event,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.860
to thank the literally dozens
and dozens of classmates

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.640
and the extraordinary staff
at the Yale Alumni Association

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.140
and Yale Conferences and Events,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.490
as well as the incredible
array of individuals

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.490
at Yale who have made this weekend

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:10.310
and the 50th anniversary
projects possible.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.370
And of course, a big thank you too,

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.300
to the members of the 50th
anniversary Planning Committee,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.650
women of the classes of 71, 72 and 73,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.103
whose names you will
find in your programs.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.800
I wanna offer heartfelt thanks as well

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.580
to our panelists and
speakers, many of whom

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.320
have traveled very far to
be with us this morning.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.530
When we sent the invitations to speak,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.040
about a year and a half
ago, almost to a person,

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.940
they responded with a resounding, yes.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.320
Clearly, something about
this anniversary moment

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.380
resonated with them and
we are enormously grateful

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.243
for their participation.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.840
People often say in an effort like this,

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.010
that it takes a village but this has been

00:01:56.010 --> 00:02:00.563
a little bit akin to a small
city, so thank you all.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.210
I also wanna mention that we have

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.170
some very special guests
in the audience today,

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.320
either this morning or
through the weekend.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.170
At the risk of leaving
out someone I really

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.840
ought to mention, I will
name only a few people here.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.970
I want to start with naming
the incomparable first lady

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.433
of Yale, Marta Moret, wife
of President Peter Salovey,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.970
who is not here this morning,
but will join us later.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.100
And I wanna acknowledge
former president, Rick Levin,

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.145
who was president of Yale for two decades.

00:02:33.145 --> 00:02:35.895
(audience claps)

00:02:38.814 --> 00:02:42.274
And Jane Levin, who has taught
legions of Yale students,

00:02:42.274 --> 00:02:45.693
(mumbles) are here today.
(audience claps)

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.060
And Linda Lorimer, retired Vice President

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.360
and former trustee of Yale, who is also,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.440
more importantly right now, my co-chair

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.392
in leading the 50 women at
Yale 150 year long effort,

00:03:01.392 --> 00:03:04.142
(audience claps)

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.380
that this gathering really has
the privilege of kicking off.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.850
And I also wanna
acknowledge Catherine Hill,

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.340
she's in the audience, the senior trustee

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.933
of the Yale Corporation, she
will be speaking on Saturday.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.253
So,
(audience claps)

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.170
I think I will stop there,

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.210
but you can recognize special guests

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.870
by their purple tags on their name tags,

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.030
you will undoubtedly remember some of them

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:36.620
from our time at Yale, and others

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.320
are people you will want to meet.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.410
So please, if you see a purple tag

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.410
or the blue tag of the speakers,

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.450
don't be afraid to
strike up a conversation.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.380
As you will see in the printed program,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.710
there's a lot to do in the next few days.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.630
There are thought provoking panels,

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:55.680
campus tours, festive receptions and meals

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.080
and time to connect with classmates.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.650
However, and above all, we urge you

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.630
to use this time on campus in the way

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.100
that is most meaningful to you.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.110
We wish you a wonderful weekend,

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.910
and a memorable experience,

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.393
and thank you for being here.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:13.830
I'd like to take a minute now

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.760
to introduce president Peter Salovey,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.000
who will offer the
weekends opening remarks

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.773
and introduce our esteemed panel.

00:04:21.407 --> 00:04:24.157
(audience claps)

00:04:27.015 --> 00:04:30.977
So, Peter Salovey is the 23rd
President of Yale University,

00:04:30.977 --> 00:04:35.120
and the Chris Argyris professor
of psychology at Yale.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.930
President Salovey first
arrived in New Haven

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:42.290
as a graduate student in
psychology in the early 1980s.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:44.403
That's when he met his wife, Marta,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.730
who at the time, was a graduate student

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:49.480
in the school of public health.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.480
For over three decades since, he and Marta

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.970
have made Yale and New Haven their home.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:57.350
And during these years, President Salovey

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:00.410
has been a teacher and
mentor to scores of students.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.510
He is also a renowned researcher

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.760
known for the development
of a broad framework

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.563
called emotional intelligence.

00:05:08.506 --> 00:05:11.256
(audience claps)

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.410
In addition, he has served the university

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.920
in many important roles, including Dean

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.200
of the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.950
Dean of Yale College and provost.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.380
Since becoming president in 2013,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.870
President Salovey has
led Yale's aspirations

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.890
to be the global research university

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:33.550
most committed to teaching and learning.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.240
Notably, during his tenure,

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.780
he opened two new residential colleges,

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.360
expanded Yale College enrollment by 15%,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.060
the biggest enrollment expansion since,

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.421
guess who came to Yale.

00:05:45.421 --> 00:05:46.490
(audience laughs)

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.380
He has also increased
access to Yale education

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.190
for students worldwide regardless

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:53.070
of their financial background.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.780
In addition, he has advanced
innovative teaching,

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.810
scholarship and research on campus

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.230
and amplified Yale's partnerships
in countries across Africa

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:05.400
and other continents around the globe.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.350
All these efforts have been in keeping

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.210
with Yale's mission to
improve the world today

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.270
and for future generations.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.010
Please join me in welcoming
President Salovey to the podium.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.760
(audience claps)

00:06:19.748 --> 00:06:24.665
- Thank you, Eve Rice, appreciate
it, thank you, thank you.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.000
Thank you, Eve, good morning, everyone.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.720
I am thrilled to see so many alumni

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.570
and friends back on campus with us today

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.360
and it is really a privilege
to officially welcome you

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.870
to the first women in Yale college,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:44.580
a 50th anniversary celebration.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.740
So as you know, this weekend

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.760
is the first event in a
year-long commemoration,

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.620
marking 50 years of
co-education in Yale college

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:00.430
and 150 years of female
students at the University.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:03.230
And so on behalf of the university,

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.990
I'd like to recognize
the dedicated volunteers

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.780
who put this weekend's program together

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.900
and who are planning
events over the course

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.810
of the entire academic year.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.810
You all know Eve Hart Rice,
Yale College class of 1973,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.860
and I just wanna add my own thank you

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.330
to her for her hard work.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.030
She is a Yale trustee, she is chair

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.430
of the 50th anniversary committee

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.550
and has worked tirelessly to put together

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.663
a truly impressive program.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:39.630
I wanna thank Linda Koch Lorimer,

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:43.580
a 1977 graduate of Yale Law School

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:45.410
and a retired vice president of

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:47.870
the University of former Yale trustee,

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:50.380
she is the co-chair of
the steering committee

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:54.900
for 50 women at Yale 150, and together,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.680
Eve and Linda, have been working with

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.470
a dedicated team of alumni
and students and faculty

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:05.880
and staff to plan this entire year.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.430
So, please join me in one more thank you

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:12.960
to Eve and to Linda and all who have made

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.960
this program possible
and this year possible.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.710
(audience claps)

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.390
So we're here to mark a moment

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:30.653
of great significance in Yale's history.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:34.140
It's a history that you lived,

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.053
it's a history that you helped make.

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:41.410
It's a story of how Yale changed forever,

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:45.053
when a group of young women
were willing to be the first.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.580
Now to be clear, there had
been women at Yale before,

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.590
but as many alumni from the graduate

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.170
and professional schools recall,

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.250
everything changed when you,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.840
the first undergraduate women, arrived.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:02.590
You changed not only your college,

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:06.100
but you changed the entire university.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.523
And it was not easy.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:09.720
There were obstacles,

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.630
there were missed opportunities,

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:12.803
there were mistakes.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:17.850
Your remarkable success
at Yale, and ever since,

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.170
is a testament to your strength,

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.170
integrity, and talent,
and it models resilience

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.453
and self-reliance for
our current students.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:33.410
In the past 50 years, Yale has continued

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:37.000
to open its doors, open its doors wider,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.050
and to push the frontiers of knowledge

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.073
and understanding further.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:46.740
One thing has led to another, today,

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.430
our students come from all 50 states

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:53.490
from over 120 countries around the world.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:58.490
The class of 2023 is the most
diverse in Yale's history.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:03.740
Over half identify as a
racial or ethnic minority,

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:07.743
and all together they speak
more than 60 languages.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:13.410
One in five members of the
first year class qualify

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.320
for Federal Pell Grants
for low income students.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.210
The number of Pell eligible students

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.433
has doubled in just five years.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.250
17% this year are the
first in their families

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:31.250
to attend college, that's a
75% increase in five years.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.660
What they share in common is a desire

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.460
to make the most of Yale's opportunities,

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.410
to engage with new ideas,
to make a difference

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:47.410
in the lives of others, to
shape the future of our world.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:52.070
And they look to you as role models,

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:56.090
they see how you forged ahead in business,

00:10:56.090 --> 00:11:00.900
law, science, the arts, many other fields,

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.703
and in a world that was not
always welcoming or accepting.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.030
They take inspiration
in your accomplishments.

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:15.030
All around us, is the Yale you
helped create 50 years ago.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.320
Because when you walked
through Phelps gate in 1969,

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:25.470
you showed us that
excellence is what matters,

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:27.950
and that it takes many different people

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:32.853
to seek light, and truth,
that is your legacy.

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:36.310
So this weekend is an opportunity,

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.880
an opportunity for the
entire Yale community,

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.900
to celebrate you, and to celebrate

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.823
the history that you helped make.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:51.840
It's a chance to hear
and record your stories,

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.690
so that we can understand
the changes you brought

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.233
to Yale and to society.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:01.090
And it's about looking ahead as well,

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:04.190
to the great chapters yet to be written

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.903
and to the history yet to be made.

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:11.190
This weekend, this celebration is but

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:14.420
a small measure of what Yale owes you.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:18.393
The trailblazers who
led Yale into a new era,

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:23.810
thank you for coming back,
thank you for being here,

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:27.733
but thank you especially
for being the first.

00:12:29.464 --> 00:12:33.510
And now, to begin our
program, what I'd like to do

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:36.380
is welcome our distinguished moderator

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.970
and panelists to the stage.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:43.570
Our moderator is Margaret
Warner, Yale class of 1971.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:46.950
Margaret is a former chief
global affairs correspondent

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:51.770
of PBS NewsHour, a former
member of the Yale Corporation,

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:55.220
and a senior fellow at
the Yale Jackson Institute

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:56.683
for global affairs.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.540
She is joined by Brenda Jubin,

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:04.560
who earned her PhD from Yale in 1973

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.280
and has served as a dean of Morse college

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:10.320
from 1970 to 1973.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:14.313
Brenda is the former
president of Brevis Press.

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:18.620
Nancy Malkiel, Professor
of History emeritus

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.520
at Princeton University and former dean

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:24.747
of the college at Princeton
is the author of the book,

00:13:24.747 --> 00:13:27.883
"Keep the Damned Women Out,"
which I hope you all have.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.760
Kurt Schmoke, Yale College class of 1971

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.750
is the president of the
University of Baltimore.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.470
Kurt is the former mayor of Baltimore,

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:41.540
former dean of Howard
University School of Law,

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:44.500
and a former member of
Yale's Board of Trustees,

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:46.540
the Yale Corporation.

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:50.460
Garry Trudeau received
his BA from Yale in 1970,

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.500
and his MFA in 1973.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:56.440
He's a Pulitzer Prize
winning author and artist

00:13:56.440 --> 00:14:00.063
and creator of the comic strips,
Bull Tales and Doonesbury.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:05.650
John Wilkinson received his
BA from Yale College in 1960,

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:09.620
and his master's degree
in education in 1963.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:12.690
John is the former associate
dean and former Dean

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:15.700
of Undergraduate affairs in Yale college

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.270
and former secretary of the university.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:22.383
So please join me in
welcoming them to the stage

00:14:24.377 --> 00:14:27.212
and welcoming every one
of you back to Yale.

00:14:27.212 --> 00:14:29.962
(audience claps)

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:49.770
- Peter, thank you, (mumbles)
fellow panelists sit.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.020
Thank you for that lovely introduction.

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:54.840
I think we're all alive
- (mumbles)

00:14:55.947 --> 00:14:59.197
(panel members mumble)

00:15:01.093 --> 00:15:01.926
- Hello

00:15:03.231 --> 00:15:04.540
- Mine's on
- You're good.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:07.220
- It's on, all right, I
just have to put it closer.

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:09.973
Thank you so much Peter for
that great introduction.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.840
All right, and I'm not in charge or sound,

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:18.100
can someone resolve this?
(audience laughs)

00:15:21.893 --> 00:15:25.143
(panel members mumble)

00:15:28.049 --> 00:15:30.770
- Well, nobody's is working.

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:32.751
- Mine is
- Yep

00:15:32.751 --> 00:15:33.584
(mumbles)

00:15:33.584 --> 00:15:34.490
- Is like more of a conversation

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:35.323
- How about that?

00:15:35.323 --> 00:15:36.940
Yes
- Okay, good.

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:37.960
- One more time Peter,

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.910
thank you for that lovely introduction,

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:41.930
and it's great to be back here.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:45.610
I remember coming here often
was at a Wednesday night

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:48.310
for the Yale Law School Film Society,

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:51.630
such as it was (laughs)
(audience claps)

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:54.560
And welcome, I'm glad
that Peter introduced

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:58.410
and let you know our panelists
and why they're all here

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.320
because they all either were part of it,

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:03.470
actually all were part of it in some way,

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:06.363
decision making and execution.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.310
So, today we're really gonna,

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:14.400
Yale had been, since 1718,
an all-male institution,

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:18.550
and then suddenly it seemed
to people on the outside,

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:21.240
that in 1968, the corporation took a vote

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:24.180
and voted to move towards co-education.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.360
So, today we're going to hear about

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.990
how that happened and how different groups

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.350
on campus felt about it.

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:32.690
And then, of course,
how it was carried out.

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:35.930
And I'm gonna start with John, nope,

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.370
I'm gonna start with Nancy, forgive me.

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:40.370
So,
(Nancy laughs)

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:42.420
So you've researched and written

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:44.280
the definitive book on co-education,

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.120
not only here, but in several other Ivy's,

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.700
Princeton and Dartmouth, for sure.

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:53.500
And, they're so, let me just get right

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:55.600
to the big question I have.

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:56.950
There's been so much mythology

00:16:56.950 --> 00:17:00.380
about why Yale voted to go coed,

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:04.272
what do you think the truth is?

00:17:04.272 --> 00:17:05.450
(audience laughs)

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:06.540
No, (mumbles)

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.170
- I'm in a story and I'm not sure I'm in

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:09.980
the business of truth, but I'll tell you

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:12.930
what I think
(audience laughs)

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:17.900
I would say there are two main reasons

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:20.630
and one background reasons.

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:25.520
The main reasons which I'll
describe are admissions,

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:30.520
number one, and competition
with Princeton, number two.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:34.197
The background is the context of the 1960s

00:17:34.197 --> 00:17:38.650
and all the changes underway in the 60s

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:40.860
that meant that by the end of the decade,

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:44.920
a place like Yale bore
only passing resemblance

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:48.310
to what had had been at the
beginning of the decade.

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:52.150
First admissions, what Yale experienced

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:55.930
in the latter part of
the 1960s like Princeton,

00:17:55.930 --> 00:18:00.140
was that the best men in high schools,

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.370
they called them the best boys,

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:04.710
these best boys no longer wanted

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:07.640
to go to all-male schools.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:10.320
This is the point at which Harvard

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:12.080
which had been going neck and neck

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:14.720
with Princeton and Yale in admissions,

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:19.350
up through the early
1960s, begins to pull away,

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:23.300
because there are girls up
the street at Radcliffe.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.340
And so Yale, like Princeton, decided

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:28.510
that it was important to admit women,

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:31.620
not because of a mission to educate women,

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:35.980
not because of a moral
commitment to equality for women,

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:39.200
but rather, because admitting women

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:42.340
was a strategic move to enable Yale,

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:46.830
like Princeton, to regain
its hold on these best boys.

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:51.000
Yale, like Princeton
couldn't abide the notion

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:56.000
that the best boys were not
any longer as interested

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:01.343
as they had been in coming to New Haven.

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:05.193
The competition with Princeton.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:09.100
Now Kingman Brewster starts this off,

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:12.290
the faculty, others at Yale, and suddenly

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:15.190
the Yale Daily News, understand

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:18.450
in the early and mid 1960s,
it would be a good thing

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:21.490
to bring undergraduate women to New Haven

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.880
but the president of the corporation

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:27.480
was certainly not having any of that.

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:29.570
However, the opportunity arose

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.300
through a mutual connection

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:35.500
for Kingman Brewster to begin exploring

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:38.160
with Alan Simpson, the
president of Vassar,

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.150
the possibility of moving Vassar,

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:43.980
from Poughkeepsie to New Haven.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:47.140
And there was about a
10-month serious study

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.500
undertaken beginning, it was announced

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:55.500
in December of 1966, very
high level president trustees,

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:59.210
faculty administrators
of what would be involved

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:02.497
in bringing Vassar to New Haven

00:20:02.497 --> 00:20:07.070
and some sort of coordinate
relationship with Yale.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:09.440
Now Bob Goheen, the
president of Princeton,

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:12.460
finds out about this and his response

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:15.060
is to go up to Sarah Lawrence College

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:18.370
and begin talking to the
president of Sarah Lawrence

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:20.800
about the possibility of doing a study

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.620
of moving Sarah Lawrence to Princeton.

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:26.370
Sarah Lawrence says, "No thank you."

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.630
Vassar eventually, of
course, said no, thank you

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:32.170
but Sarah Lawrence says, not even a study.

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:36.360
Princeton's more serious
response to Vassar-Yale

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.620
was to commission a study of,

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:44.120
a data driven analytic
study of the feasibility

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:47.680
and desirability of
co-education at Princeton,

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.590
a study undertaken by Gardner Patterson,

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:53.840
an economist in the Woodrow Wilson School.

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:58.840
So, after Vassar, November
of 1967 says no, thank you

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:03.607
to Yale, Brewster says,
"Well, we'll establish

00:21:03.607 --> 00:21:05.870
"our own coordinate college for women."

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:09.200
And he spends the next 10 months musing

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:11.420
about what that might entail.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.770
For Yale undergraduate, that's one option,

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:15.900
there were a number of others

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:18.380
and he wrote some position papers,

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:21.720
but essentially, nothing was happening.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:25.840
Now the Patterson report, is completed,

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.010
published in the Princeton alumni weekly

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:31.190
at the end of September 1968.

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:34.930
A copy comes to Brewster,
he reads it and he sees

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:39.700
that Princeton could be
serious about co-education.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:43.860
So, from the end of September
till the middle of November

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:48.410
with no process and no
planning, Brewster gets

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:51.210
the Yale Corporation and the Yale faculty

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:55.310
to vote in favor of co-education
for September of 1969.

00:21:57.560 --> 00:21:59.130
Now, Princeton at this point

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:01.780
is going through it's very deliberate,

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:05.053
process-oriented consultation

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.070
with alumni all around the country.

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:12.470
In January of 67, the
Princeton trustees vote

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:15.060
for co-education in principle,

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:18.380
and they tell president
Goheen to come back

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:21.140
when he's ready and tell
them how they're going,

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:22.370
how they would like to do it.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:26.300
And he does that in late April of 1969

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:27.320
at which point, of course,

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:30.030
Yale has already made its decisions

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:33.810
on admits to the class of 73.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:37.960
Now, if Princeton had done what
Princeton would normally do,

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.630
the Princeton trustees in late April,

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:44.090
would have said we're going
to embark on co-education,

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:47.280
we'll spend a lot of time
getting organized for it,

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:49.750
and we'll do it for September of 1970.

00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:51.710
But they couldn't do
that, because the women

00:22:51.710 --> 00:22:55.380
were coming to Yale in September of 1969.

00:22:55.380 --> 00:23:00.290
And so in late April of
1969, Princeton decides

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:05.260
to bring women on in the fall of 1969.

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:08.290
- Let me give (mumbles)
John Wilkinson in on this

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:10.820
because you're gonna take
us behind the scenes.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:14.160
- Well, I agree with Nancy completely.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.640
Yale had no plan, and as a consequence,

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:20.690
had a flawed process, and we did it

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:24.780
because the rumor in New Haven
was that Princeton, in fact,

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:28.670
was gonna go co-educational
in the fall of 1969.

00:23:28.670 --> 00:23:31.550
Now, I agree with Nancy's assessment

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:33.660
of some of the reasons but there are

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.460
a couple others that I think
were really very important.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:41.310
We, then, had a student
body which had become,

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:44.344
it reached its maturity,
its majority began

00:23:44.344 --> 00:23:47.630
(mumbles) 18 rather than 21.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:49.910
The Yale students at that
period were not going

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:51.640
to let the administration tell them

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:53.870
how they were (mumbles) live their lives.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:56.386
And the one thing that
the Yale students wanted,

00:23:56.386 --> 00:23:58.520
(mumbles) all those men,

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:01.183
they wanted Yale to be co-educational.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:06.480
And the students drove that with coed week

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:10.750
and demonstrations and
in grading assistance,

00:24:10.750 --> 00:24:14.230
and part of that reason,
I'm convinced was,

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:18.570
Yale was in the process in
its efforts to diversify,

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.780
had discovered public
schools in a big way.

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:24.670
And we went from two
thirds private boarding

00:24:24.670 --> 00:24:27.110
to two thirds public.

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:29.460
All of those public school students,

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:31.220
and a lot of the day students

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:33.400
had gone to co-educational institutions.

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:35.900
They had never encountered
single-sex education

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:37.520
until they arrived at Yale.

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.980
It was not something
with a regard as normal

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:42.233
or natural or acceptable.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:47.130
Now, I wanna add one thing
and Nancy mentions this

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:50.874
in her book, and I should
say, if you haven't read it,

00:24:50.874 --> 00:24:51.806
get it
- It's great

00:24:51.806 --> 00:24:53.720
and read it, it's a fantastic,

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:56.770
it's not just Yale and the Ivy's,

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:59.197
it's also the Seven Sisters, it deals

00:24:59.197 --> 00:25:01.680
with those institutions which already

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:04.910
had coordinate colleges, and
it has a wonderful section

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:09.030
on Oxford and Cambridge
and how they approach this.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.050
And the other book I wanna recommend

00:25:11.050 --> 00:25:12.752
is, Anne Perkins here?
- She's here.

00:25:12.752 --> 00:25:15.824
(audience claps)

00:25:15.824 --> 00:25:19.380
- It just came out and
it's a wonderful book,

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:21.800
and if you are have an abiding interest

00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:24.170
in how these decisions were made,

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:26.456
these are the two books to read.

00:25:26.456 --> 00:25:29.450
Now, one of the things that struck me

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:31.790
as I read
(mumbles)

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:34.090
One of the things that struck me

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:39.090
as I read Nancy's book was
that a whole institutions

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:43.850
became co-educational
for pragmatic reasons.

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:46.580
These were not principal decisions.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.860
The only
(audience mumbles)

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.940
These were not principal decisions,

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:54.700
these were pragmatic decisions.

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:59.070
And, I don't know of any president

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:03.810
of any College or University
who actually made the statement

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:08.643
that sir Eric Ashby of Clare
College, Cambridge, said,

00:26:11.497 --> 00:26:15.437
"The motivating force behind
the college's willingness

00:26:15.437 --> 00:26:17.417
"to read on the issue," he said,

00:26:17.417 --> 00:26:22.417
"What's the (mumbles) inequality
and injustice being done?"

00:26:25.578 --> 00:26:29.041
This was not a statement was being made

00:26:29.041 --> 00:26:32.730
by the United States, it
was made in Cambridge.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:34.170
That's (mumbles)

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:39.170
- Interesting, so, Garry
and Kurt for undergraduates,

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:43.550
you were in the transition,
Garry, class of 70,

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.300
myself, I'm sorry, myself also

00:26:46.300 --> 00:26:48.323
with Kurt in the class of 71,

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:51.050
I know that I can't ask you to speak

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:53.170
for all men undergraduates,
(audience laughs)

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:55.170
but, it's early (mumbles) fair,

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:56.903
but I'm going to, Garry,
I'll start with you

00:26:56.903 --> 00:26:59.330
because you were one class ahead.

00:26:59.330 --> 00:27:02.170
Was it as John describes?

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:04.980
That there was this groundswell of,

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:09.145
from where were you sat
and sketched and wrote and

00:27:09.145 --> 00:27:12.096
(audience laughs)

00:27:12.096 --> 00:27:13.520
- Was there a groundswell among

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.150
the student population?
- (mumbles)

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:19.970
- Absolutely, and one of the
most remarkable precursors

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:24.970
to women actually coming
to Yale, as real students

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:29.130
was when they came as fake
students for coed week.

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:31.963
And that was organized
by an undergraduate.

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:35.370
When you think of the
administration involved

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:39.030
in bringing hundreds of girls
and clearing out our rooms

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:40.260
and making them available to women,

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:42.560
it's quite a remarkable achievement,

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:45.520
but it really put a stake in the ground.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:47.690
And all of us, particularly,

00:27:47.690 --> 00:27:50.660
I was coming off my 10th
year of single-sex education,

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:54.970
so, it was quite a revelation to me.

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:57.530
And I was so taken by it that I went

00:27:57.530 --> 00:28:01.940
to Wellesley's coed week
where I was one of 50 men,

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:05.230
just to see what it'd be
like to be in the minority.

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:08.640
And my first class, there was an art class

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:11.663
where I was asked to be the model

00:28:11.663 --> 00:28:14.496
(audience laughs)

00:28:16.733 --> 00:28:18.870
So, I had a little taste
of what it was like

00:28:18.870 --> 00:28:23.870
to be a curiosity before the
women did when they came,

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:29.100
but I was also from my little bubble

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:33.530
of prep school privilege,
I was perfectly prepared

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:35.460
to go through my 11th year

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:40.460
of single-sex education without complaint.

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:45.390
But, when it became known that
it was actually gonna happen,

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:47.830
I think that, a great many of us thought,

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:51.570
well, this is a new feature for Yale.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:55.340
It's not, I, maybe folks like Kurt

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:57.930
who actually joined the
co-education committee

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:01.510
and were, and mainly involved
had a different view,

00:29:01.510 --> 00:29:05.970
but I think most of us really saw it

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:09.096
in terms of new dating opportunities.

00:29:09.096 --> 00:29:09.929
(audience laughs)

00:29:09.929 --> 00:29:13.223
And, it was something that,

00:29:15.070 --> 00:29:16.563
happened all at once.

00:29:18.732 --> 00:29:20.210
I sometimes think of co-education Yale's

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:22.543
a little bit like the Affordable Care Act,

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:26.530
it was something that
was debated for decades,

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:29.770
and then it was rushed forward all at once

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:32.610
and had a very bumpy rollout.

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:36.930
And then once it happened,
and was a success,

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:40.430
everybody's was angry on both sides,

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:42.410
because it was a half measure.

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:46.390
And people became really
angry very quickly,

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:49.700
by, halfway through
- The men and women.

00:29:49.700 --> 00:29:54.700
- Men and women, by the small number and,

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.210
and I think the issue of social equity

00:29:57.210 --> 00:30:01.850
that Yale had a responsibility
to educate women

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:05.280
just out of fairness and
for the good of society,

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:09.280
that only kind of arrived, I would say,

00:30:09.280 --> 00:30:11.550
and I'm only speaking from my one year,

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:15.210
but that sort of began to
become a very important issue

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:17.900
about halfway through that
first year of education.

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:19.820
- [Margaret] And Kurt, what about you?

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:22.310
- Well, Margaret, let me first start

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:25.660
by noting there in our alma mater,

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:27.653
there's a line that says,

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:33.139
how bright will seem
through memory's haze.

00:30:33.139 --> 00:30:36.460
(audience laughs)

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:40.136
On this matter, there is more
haze than brightness for me.

00:30:40.136 --> 00:30:42.886
(audience claps)

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:48.680
But it was because of reading Nancy's book

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:53.340
and Anne back there, that I started

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:56.670
to have my memory refreshed.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:31:00.450
And as Garry noted, I
was actually appointed

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:04.420
to the first Planning Committee.

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:05.810
It was called the planning committee

00:31:05.810 --> 00:31:10.810
for co-education headed by
Elga Wasserman at the time,

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:16.074
and, so you can blame me (laughs)

00:31:16.074 --> 00:31:19.870
for some of the bumps that
occurred in the early years,

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:24.870
but, Yale was going through
quite a dramatic, for Yale,

00:31:25.890 --> 00:31:28.600
a dramatic period of transition.

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:33.253
At the time, as John
mentioned, in the 1950s here,

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.020
what passed for diversity was

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:40.529
a white male public school
student from the Midwest.

00:31:40.529 --> 00:31:43.362
(audience laughs)

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:50.040
So then, in the 60s, the early 60s,

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:55.000
there were a few African
American male students

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:58.950
on campus and I think,
although Sam Chauncey,

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:01.430
though you'll hear from
my guest at the later,

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:05.140
will probably explain, I wasn't sure

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:09.210
how I got appointed to the committee,

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:12.543
co-education Planning
Committee, I was a sophomore

00:32:12.543 --> 00:32:16.740
at the time, honestly, I don't
remember but I think it was

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:20.070
because and Anne could
probably give you more detail

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:22.550
on this, that there was some sense

00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:27.420
that an African American male,

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:31.680
having gone through a cultural change

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:36.300
or lived through a
cultural change on campus

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:41.300
might in some way give an indication,

00:32:41.320 --> 00:32:44.610
although with race and
gender very different issues,

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:46.990
but that might in some way add

00:32:48.224 --> 00:32:51.270
to an insight about the
change that was going

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:53.710
to occur to kind of, how do you adapt

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:58.050
to a cultural change, it's
gonna occur on campus?

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.200
Garry is absolutely right though that

00:33:01.540 --> 00:33:04.970
for most of the men here,
was overwhelming support.

00:33:04.970 --> 00:33:07.210
I mean, there was very few,

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:11.350
certainly no organized opposition to this.

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:16.280
There was, my recollection,
some irreverent moments

00:33:17.530 --> 00:33:21.823
if some of you may remember
the Yale marching band,

00:33:25.150 --> 00:33:28.230
one time did their own statement

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:33.230
about the potential merger
between Yale and Vassar.

00:33:35.059 --> 00:33:36.657
If you
- I forgot (mumbles)

00:33:36.657 --> 00:33:38.141
- He forgot about that, okay.

00:33:38.141 --> 00:33:39.710
(audience laughs)

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:43.200
Maybe I won't explain it.
- (mumbles)

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:46.000
- But in any event, it
was an irreverent moment

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:49.910
that, where they commented
on it, but the bottom line

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:53.310
is that most of the men
were very supportive.

00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:56.380
And we did, I think the notes from

00:33:56.380 --> 00:34:00.150
the planning committee show that,

00:34:00.150 --> 00:34:01.130
generally, my votes who were

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:04.800
on the right side of history
- (mumbles) hear that

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:07.540
- There was an argument at one time

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:11.340
in the early planning
process about establishing

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:15.010
a separate college at Yale for women

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:17.270
rather than having women in all

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:21.420
of the colleges and that was voted down.

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:25.020
But a lot of debate, a lot of planning,

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:29.460
and I think the decisions that
we made were the right one,

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:31.630
though the road was bumpy,

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:34.370
- And some might disagree
about the preparation,

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:38.760
but I want to get Miss Brenda in this.

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:45.240
Excuse me, Ana Brenda Dubin, so you were

00:34:45.240 --> 00:34:50.240
a young philosophy instructor,
and you taught both men

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:53.130
and then when it went coed, women

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:56.890
and you were named the first woman ever

00:34:56.890 --> 00:34:59.530
to be the dean of a residential college.

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:03.363
So how did it, once the women arrived,

00:35:04.210 --> 00:35:08.060
what change did you
detect both as a teacher

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:13.060
and as, sort of, dorm
counselor and enforcer?

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:18.000
- Let me start back one step,

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:20.053
I was also a graduate student at Yale.

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:23.360
And graduate students at that point

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:25.640
were really second class citizens.

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:28.189
Yale College was the
center of the universe,

00:35:28.189 --> 00:35:33.189
the graduate school was
definitely peripheral

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:36.620
to the main thrust of the university.

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:41.550
Within the graduate school,
women were even worse off.

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:43.800
We were all corralled into (mumbles)

00:35:43.800 --> 00:35:48.470
that architectural
masterpiece, and we're told,

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:50.460
among other things that
we had to wear skirts

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:55.460
to go to the library,
which most women found

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:59.900
a little bit off, but that was

00:35:59.900 --> 00:36:02.680
the Yale University graduate school.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:06.380
When Yale became coed,
and when I was named dean,

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:09.860
I became part of an entirely new universe.

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.820
This was a community, a vibrant community

00:36:12.820 --> 00:36:14.120
of men and women, yes, of course,

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:16.953
there were bumps in the road, and Yale,

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:21.200
and there were a few men who wished me not

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:23.890
to ever have been appointed,
thank you very much.

00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:28.890
But in general, it was
a vibrant, lively place.

00:36:29.380 --> 00:36:32.610
And I am personally very
grateful for co-education

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:35.750
because without that, I would
probably still have been

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:41.110
a third, fourth class citizen
in the graduate school.

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:44.780
Okay, now, when women came.

00:36:44.780 --> 00:36:47.573
Yeah, I taught in the
philosophy department.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:51.790
My first class with a
woman, I had one woman

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:56.203
in a class of maybe 20,
and I thought, oh, dear,

00:36:57.130 --> 00:36:59.580
I sort of hoped that she's
a really good student,

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:04.780
and she wasn't, and I
thought, well, that's too bad,

00:37:04.780 --> 00:37:08.920
just get over it, and, she's
gonna be an average student

00:37:08.920 --> 00:37:11.010
and why not have an average woman?

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:14.520
Not every woman has to be, super woman.

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:18.320
But, in general, I didn't have problems

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:19.420
on the teaching front.

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:23.240
As far as being dean was concerned,

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:25.370
I wish I had held all women in the sense

00:37:25.370 --> 00:37:27.514
that it would have freed up so much time.

00:37:27.514 --> 00:37:28.880
(audience laughs)

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:31.333
I spent all my time dealing with the men.

00:37:32.315 --> 00:37:34.240
The men had all the problems.
- (mumbles)

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:36.480
- The men needed the dean's excuses,

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:39.700
the men, needed that recommendation
for Harvard Law School,

00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:41.180
which they were never going to get into

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:43.923
but daddy said they had to apply anyway.

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:48.570
So, this was basically my experience,

00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:50.180
in a nutshell (mumbles)

00:37:50.180 --> 00:37:51.880
- The men needed more hand holding

00:37:51.880 --> 00:37:53.720
and guidance than the women?

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:57.090
- Much much more, much much more.

00:37:57.090 --> 00:37:58.580
The women who came in, first of all,

00:37:58.580 --> 00:38:00.650
of course, they were hand picked.

00:38:00.650 --> 00:38:04.420
And, so you had women who were strong

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:08.890
and bright and whatnot,
but they got there,

00:38:08.890 --> 00:38:13.890
and, for instance, in Morse,
we had the strangest assortment

00:38:14.050 --> 00:38:16.670
of women living together in an entry way.

00:38:16.670 --> 00:38:20.270
How they did ever get along was beside me,

00:38:20.270 --> 00:38:22.240
but they did, they got along fine.

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:24.470
Nobody came saying, "I
have to move from this room

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:27.087
"because I can't stand X and Y."

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:29.807
The men on the other hand, was like,

00:38:29.807 --> 00:38:32.897
"I have to remove, because he's too noisy,

00:38:32.897 --> 00:38:35.567
"and I can't stand him anymore."

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:41.420
So, as I said, I would
have had all the free time

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:43.550
in the world, my dissertation
would have been finished

00:38:43.550 --> 00:38:44.690
well (mumbles)
- Years before.

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:46.301
- If I had only women.

00:38:46.301 --> 00:38:49.184
(audience laughs)
(audience claps)

00:38:49.184 --> 00:38:51.330
- [Margaret] So, Garry (mumbles)

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:55.740
- Can I comment on the super woman label

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:57.790
that the women got tagged with?

00:38:57.790 --> 00:38:59.660
I think,
(audience mumbles)

00:38:59.660 --> 00:39:01.810
- Guess you have to put the
mic right up in front you.

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:03.630
- Okay, I have to find that sweet spot.

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:05.133
Is that okay?

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:09.360
I just wanted to comment
on the super women label

00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:12.914
that, who gave it to,
it was a Time magazine.

00:39:12.914 --> 00:39:13.747
(mumbles)

00:39:13.747 --> 00:39:17.913
And, it was initially
a little intimidating

00:39:17.913 --> 00:39:22.103
from a male perspective,
but once the women arrived,

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:27.410
even though it was an infusion
of high IQ individuals

00:39:27.410 --> 00:39:32.400
what was most disruptive
to the male culture

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:37.400
was the high EQ that,
the women just seemed

00:39:37.830 --> 00:39:39.924
more developed, mentally mature

00:39:39.924 --> 00:39:42.250
(audience laughs)

00:39:42.250 --> 00:39:46.500
I always had the sense that
they could see right through me

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:50.863
and it wasn't a pretty
picture, and so it was,

00:39:52.310 --> 00:39:55.423
that was the only way in
which I thought they were,

00:39:56.380 --> 00:39:58.890
actually over time, I thought that was

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:01.913
a tremendous contribution
to the overall culture.

00:40:03.705 --> 00:40:04.538
(mumbles)

00:40:04.538 --> 00:40:06.623
- But difficult for some men, Kurt?

00:40:08.103 --> 00:40:10.760
Oh, I'm sorry, but difficult for some men,

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.230
do you agree Kurt?

00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:17.230
- Well, it's hard to
generalize because the,

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:20.020
our experiences were so different though.

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:22.470
When I came in, I came
from a public high school

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:25.593
that was all-male, 4000 all boys,

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:28.260
yeah, and it was kinda weird,

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:32.850
but, so this was not the
shocking experience to me

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:37.290
and also my experience
here was slightly different

00:40:37.290 --> 00:40:38.630
than some of my other colleagues

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:41.420
because I came in as a football player.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:45.853
So, that was a traditional
Yale institution.

00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:49.230
So I didn't feel a
disconnect from the place,

00:40:49.230 --> 00:40:53.500
the way some of my other colleagues did.

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:56.717
And with respect to the women,

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:01.980
well, I can see the
Congresswoman coming in now.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:05.560
I mean, I know that there definitely

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:07.610
were some high powered people,

00:41:07.610 --> 00:41:10.390
but I didn't get a sense of that.

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:11.760
Maybe I didn't because I didn't read

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:13.770
Time magazine at the time,

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:15.400
- [Margaret] New York
Times or whatever it was.

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:19.423
- But I did get a real sense
of friendship and camaraderie.

00:41:20.620 --> 00:41:24.083
And, not a disconnect
that some others felt.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:25.973
- So, John
- (mumbles)

00:41:25.973 --> 00:41:28.770
- Oh, yes, go right ahead,
I was gonna (mumbles)

00:41:28.770 --> 00:41:33.770
- I agree, the men were
accustomed to being pampered

00:41:33.860 --> 00:41:35.840
and they came to the Yale
College dean's office

00:41:35.840 --> 00:41:38.530
all the time, asking for help.

00:41:38.530 --> 00:41:42.440
The women who came, came to
tell us how to do our job.

00:41:42.440 --> 00:41:47.440
(audience laughs)
(audience claps)

00:41:49.091 --> 00:41:53.787
And, I remember the first time
I had an undergraduate woman,

00:41:53.787 --> 00:41:57.950
had a tear roll down her
cheek, she was so embarrassed,

00:41:57.950 --> 00:42:00.700
and I pulled out a
Kleenex and I said I have

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:03.810
about 10 boxes of Kleenex
here I've been using

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:07.763
for years for men, don't
apologize for tears.

00:42:07.763 --> 00:42:10.268
(Margaret laughs)
(audience claps)

00:42:10.268 --> 00:42:13.410
I don't think the women had any more

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:14.870
or fewer problems than the men.

00:42:14.870 --> 00:42:18.410
I think the women were much more reluctant

00:42:18.410 --> 00:42:20.453
to express them
- Could well have been.

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:23.781
- And I think that was
part of the culture.

00:42:23.781 --> 00:42:25.010
(mumbles)

00:42:25.010 --> 00:42:28.420
I think that women were much less willing

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:31.330
to express their issues and their problems

00:42:31.330 --> 00:42:34.200
than the men because they had come to this

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:37.873
new strange place and
they had to be resilient.

00:42:38.887 --> 00:42:40.193
(audience claps)
And they were.

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:45.630
- So again, John, and also Nancy,

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:49.100
what can you tell us about
the selection process?

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:51.140
I mean, over grades or something,

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:52.930
but what was, I've heard, again,

00:42:52.930 --> 00:42:55.640
there's a lot of mythology
about what Elga Wasserman

00:42:55.640 --> 00:42:57.453
was looking for in particular.

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:01.400
Can anyone here shed light on it?

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:06.400
- Well, what Elga and Sam
Chauncey clearly agreed on

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:11.540
was that they were looking
for something beyond

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:15.630
the credentials you might normally expect

00:43:15.630 --> 00:43:20.250
in terms of academic
achievement and leadership

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:23.230
in high school, they
were looking for people

00:43:23.230 --> 00:43:25.500
who were tough, who were gritty,

00:43:25.500 --> 00:43:28.800
who were resilient, who could in fact,

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:32.060
survive in this environment.

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:34.187
And they were very explicit about that.

00:43:34.187 --> 00:43:37.410
And both of them played a significant role

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:42.410
in the review of applications
for women at Princeton,

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:46.213
the phrase that was often used was,

00:43:47.270 --> 00:43:51.480
people who had been American
field service students abroad

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:55.550
was one marker for the
kind of grit and ability

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:59.750
to adjust to unfamiliar circumstances.

00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:04.750
The theory as enunciated
by Wasserman and Chauncey

00:44:04.790 --> 00:44:07.720
was it is really gonna
take a lot of strength

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:12.720
and adaptability for women to
manage at this institution.

00:44:13.090 --> 00:44:15.600
- Yeah, Marg, I think the analogy

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:19.680
that I had to the
process was the selection

00:44:19.680 --> 00:44:22.920
of Jackie Robinson, as
the first African American

00:44:22.920 --> 00:44:26.100
in Major League Baseball,
that Branch Rickey,

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:29.340
the owner of the team, wasn't just looking

00:44:29.340 --> 00:44:32.800
for the best athlete,
there was a combination

00:44:32.800 --> 00:44:35.940
of characteristics that they wanted,

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:39.020
and somebody who was
gonna be able to stand up

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:43.360
to a tough environment as
well as being a great athlete.

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:46.030
So they were definitely looking for,

00:44:46.030 --> 00:44:48.240
outstanding academic achievement,

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:52.170
but it was that plus some characteristic.

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:54.660
- We're all gonna be
blushing in this auditorium.

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:57.750
But anyway, Garry, you
wanted to say something.

00:44:57.750 --> 00:45:00.423
Oh, you just looked very, very interested.

00:45:01.260 --> 00:45:03.440
So John, anyone chime in on this,

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:07.960
what was the thinking behind
the lack of preparation?

00:45:07.960 --> 00:45:11.380
I mean, I certainly
(audience laughs)

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:12.673
Was there thinking?

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:19.170
I mean, we girls are women we would have,

00:45:19.170 --> 00:45:21.710
there were no women's bathroom say,

00:45:21.710 --> 00:45:23.900
in this, well there probably
was in this building

00:45:23.900 --> 00:45:27.580
but in most buildings, so you
had to run back from class

00:45:27.580 --> 00:45:31.570
to your dorm room or
suite to use the bathroom.

00:45:31.570 --> 00:45:34.590
The women who were very
athletically minded,

00:45:34.590 --> 00:45:36.790
were just stunned.

00:45:36.790 --> 00:45:38.660
I think Anne Perkins has a great anecdote

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:40.720
about this in her book.

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:42.680
The young woman comes in and says,

00:45:42.680 --> 00:45:46.210
well, so I wanted to join the hockey team,

00:45:46.210 --> 00:45:49.750
and the varsity and the
Director of Athletics

00:45:49.750 --> 00:45:53.110
just looked dumbfounded, there've
been no arrangements made

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:57.000
for or anticipation that women athletes

00:45:57.000 --> 00:46:01.907
had the same aspirations as
men and would need facilities

00:46:02.860 --> 00:46:06.760
that would serve them if they were going

00:46:06.760 --> 00:46:08.590
to be the superstars they wanted to be.

00:46:08.590 --> 00:46:10.673
So what was the thinking?

00:46:11.558 --> 00:46:14.441
(audience laughs)

00:46:14.441 --> 00:46:18.070
- There was, there (mumbles)
been a (mumbles) thinking.

00:46:18.070 --> 00:46:21.610
My wife as a wealthy classmate who became

00:46:21.610 --> 00:46:26.177
a professor at the University
of SUNY, Stony Brook,

00:46:27.740 --> 00:46:32.270
who went to Oxford several years ago

00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:34.980
before they had become co-educational,

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:39.814
and the fellows voted to
make her an honorary man.

00:46:39.814 --> 00:46:41.100
(audience laughs)

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:44.900
So she could attend those
events that the fellows,

00:46:44.900 --> 00:46:47.780
all of them were male, attended.

00:46:47.780 --> 00:46:50.020
In some, I thought, (mumbles)
told this story to us

00:46:50.020 --> 00:46:52.840
a few weeks ago, I thought,
the lack of planning,

00:46:52.840 --> 00:46:55.760
we brought in 500 women
and we treated them

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:58.282
like honorary men.

00:46:58.282 --> 00:46:59.115
(audience laughs)

00:46:59.115 --> 00:47:03.264
The planning took place,
largely, I'm sorry, Kurt,

00:47:03.264 --> 00:47:06.503
a lot of the planning took
place after you were here.

00:47:08.013 --> 00:47:12.740
Because it was a rush job,
and we probably should

00:47:12.740 --> 00:47:14.980
have had a couple years of planning.

00:47:14.980 --> 00:47:17.450
But I don't think the
undergraduate student body

00:47:17.450 --> 00:47:19.350
at that time would have permitted

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:21.630
the administration to wait two years.

00:47:21.630 --> 00:47:26.630
- But Nancy didn't (mumbles)
actually set the terms

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:28.720
when you said, this is our R&amp;D year,

00:47:28.720 --> 00:47:32.313
this is an experiment and--

00:47:32.313 --> 00:47:35.669
- [Margaret] Garry, can
you explain more clearly.

00:47:35.669 --> 00:47:40.100
- (mumbles) my understanding
was that Brewster viewed,

00:47:40.100 --> 00:47:43.740
the first year of
co-education as the R&amp;D year

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:48.740
that it was the year in which
he expected to experiment and,

00:47:52.280 --> 00:47:53.340
correct me if I'm wrong, Kurt,

00:47:53.340 --> 00:47:55.330
but I think that the
co-education committee

00:47:55.330 --> 00:47:58.030
was supposed to be
doing the field research

00:47:58.030 --> 00:48:00.800
and gathering the data and
finding out what was good

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:02.530
and what was bad and then
reporting back to him.

00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:06.070
The problem was he didn't do
anything with that report.

00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:08.670
So, it was another two or three years

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:12.200
before we were on the
path to full education.

00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:14.460
- To full (mumbles) what
do you wanna add, Nancy?

00:48:14.460 --> 00:48:17.050
- Well, if Brewster thought about this,

00:48:17.050 --> 00:48:19.407
I haven't found it in the written record.

00:48:19.407 --> 00:48:22.240
(audience laughs)

00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:28.260
I think he just figured,
Yale was going to do this

00:48:28.260 --> 00:48:32.360
and it would work itself out some way

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:34.870
or another and the women would get here

00:48:34.870 --> 00:48:37.530
and would help Yale
figure out how to do this.

00:48:37.530 --> 00:48:41.300
But as Garry said, he was not interested

00:48:41.300 --> 00:48:45.410
in the reports from the
co-education committee

00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:49.000
and so it was just work itself out.

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:54.000
- Yeah, and I think in both
Nancy's and Anne's book

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:58.150
that is demonstrated by
the tension that occurred

00:48:58.150 --> 00:49:01.313
between Kingman Brewster
and Elva Wasserman,

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:06.370
she tried her best not only to get

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:11.204
these reports directly to
him, but to elevate her status

00:49:11.204 --> 00:49:16.204
at the university, and
he continued to keep her

00:49:16.250 --> 00:49:19.850
a special assistant rather
than as an associate dean

00:49:19.850 --> 00:49:24.370
or as a dean, or as a
senior vice president,

00:49:24.370 --> 00:49:27.700
and so there was a tension there.

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:31.320
The other thing that's not really captured

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:34.250
in anything that I've read, the pressure

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:39.250
that he was on under by
alumni and some trustees

00:49:39.330 --> 00:49:41.063
who were against this.

00:49:42.070 --> 00:49:45.780
He had the votes on the corporation

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:48.310
at one time with some major leaders

00:49:48.310 --> 00:49:50.620
to move this thing forward, but there was

00:49:50.620 --> 00:49:55.620
some real opposition and a question about

00:49:55.820 --> 00:50:00.820
what that might mean for
the future financial status

00:50:01.100 --> 00:50:04.630
of the place and I know
that that was a concern

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:09.193
that he had, let's do it now
and, ask forgiveness later.

00:50:10.150 --> 00:50:11.940
- [Margaret] Yes, Nancy.

00:50:11.940 --> 00:50:14.290
- Which is in a way characteristic

00:50:14.290 --> 00:50:18.970
of the way Brewster
operated on other friends.

00:50:18.970 --> 00:50:23.540
Would a really,
deliberate, careful planner

00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:26.410
have made the remark he made about

00:50:26.410 --> 00:50:29.150
the unlikelihood of a
black revolutionary getting

00:50:29.150 --> 00:50:32.800
a fair trial in the United States?

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:35.770
Would he have made the remarks he made

00:50:35.770 --> 00:50:39.660
about his opposition to the Vietnam War?

00:50:39.660 --> 00:50:42.950
Probably not, if he was being careful

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:46.260
and working not to stir up opposition,

00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:50.700
but Brewster was under
attack from so many quarters

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:53.740
over his stance on the war, stance

00:50:53.740 --> 00:50:57.600
on the Panther's willingness
to open the campus

00:50:57.600 --> 00:51:02.600
to the demonstrators on May
Day over his unwillingness

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:07.590
to silence or fire William Sloane Coffin.

00:51:07.590 --> 00:51:10.260
So co-education was just one other thing

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:12.630
where people were really angry at him.

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:16.260
And my sense is that
because of the alumni anger

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:19.940
at Brewster on so many fronts, his ability

00:51:19.940 --> 00:51:23.350
to maneuver on co-education
was more limited

00:51:23.350 --> 00:51:27.020
than it might have been if
he had had a freer hand.

00:51:27.020 --> 00:51:29.940
- Well, give us a sense of the alumni.

00:51:29.940 --> 00:51:31.680
I guess I'll turn to you, John, again.

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:33.400
How fierce was the opposition?

00:51:33.400 --> 00:51:34.660
What form did it take?

00:51:34.660 --> 00:51:37.810
What was really the overriding objection?

00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:41.222
- Well, because of the lack of planning,

00:51:41.222 --> 00:51:45.540
and the immediacy of the decision,

00:51:45.540 --> 00:51:48.113
I think those who were,

00:51:49.630 --> 00:51:51.390
very much in favor of co-education

00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:53.380
were very pleasantly surprised.

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:55.570
Those who were opposed to co-education,

00:51:55.570 --> 00:51:58.350
were very unpleasantly surprised

00:51:58.350 --> 00:52:03.350
and the opposition was
more imagined than real.

00:52:04.010 --> 00:52:07.980
It didn't take very long for our alumni

00:52:07.980 --> 00:52:11.890
to think that in fact, they had daughters

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:14.330
who were going to get into Yale.

00:52:14.330 --> 00:52:18.160
And I understand full
well, why the presidents

00:52:18.160 --> 00:52:20.030
of these institutions were afraid of that

00:52:20.030 --> 00:52:22.740
because sometimes the
opposition was very vocal.

00:52:22.740 --> 00:52:25.213
But indeed, the opposition
was not very deep.

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:28.493
- [Margaret] And did it have a theme?

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:31.443
- Well, it was hard to sort it out at Yale

00:52:31.443 --> 00:52:34.730
because as Nancy said, there
were lots of other issues.

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:36.330
We were dealing with Panthers,

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:39.380
we were dealing with the Vietnam War,

00:52:39.380 --> 00:52:44.210
we were dealing with the
changes in the admissions,

00:52:44.210 --> 00:52:46.890
decisions, the changing the demography

00:52:46.890 --> 00:52:48.840
of the undergraduate student body,

00:52:48.840 --> 00:52:53.230
so that, there were a
number of people coming out

00:52:53.230 --> 00:52:55.250
of that hornet's nest mad as hell

00:52:55.250 --> 00:52:57.450
at Kingman for a whole range of decisions,

00:52:57.450 --> 00:52:59.555
co-education was only one of them.

00:52:59.555 --> 00:53:03.180
- And did it (mumbles)
after a year or two?

00:53:03.180 --> 00:53:05.940
- Yes, thanks to the
leadership of people like,

00:53:05.940 --> 00:53:08.640
Fred Rose and Mel (mumbles)
and there were a number

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:10.670
of alumni who stepped up to the front

00:53:10.670 --> 00:53:13.949
and they really took over, and

00:53:13.949 --> 00:53:15.298
(audience claps)

00:53:15.298 --> 00:53:19.070
And created the association
to be all alumni

00:53:19.070 --> 00:53:21.280
which became comprehensive and represent

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:24.000
the entire university and
go to graduate student,

00:53:25.558 --> 00:53:28.363
and has flourished as a very
supportive organization.

00:53:29.794 --> 00:53:33.130
- (mumbles) can I, I just
want to say, you know this,

00:53:33.130 --> 00:53:36.560
you have put this in
context about the change.

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:41.120
Yale, look, I think that Yale didn't have

00:53:41.120 --> 00:53:45.563
its first Jewish Corporation
member until late 60s.

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:50.840
So, this place and when I was here,

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:54.060
I was still listening to some
old blues that were talking

00:53:54.060 --> 00:53:57.566
about Yale admitting too
many people from New York.

00:53:57.566 --> 00:54:00.030
(audience laughs)

00:54:00.030 --> 00:54:01.743
Which was code, you know.

00:54:02.890 --> 00:54:07.890
And so, there was serious opposition

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:13.030
to this kind of change going on.

00:54:13.030 --> 00:54:15.720
So I think that that really
has to be placed in context.

00:54:15.720 --> 00:54:19.220
It seems like kind of an easy,

00:54:19.220 --> 00:54:21.410
but hasty decision that he made,

00:54:21.410 --> 00:54:26.230
but it was pretty
dramatic and revolutionary

00:54:26.230 --> 00:54:29.930
for this place that was
tied into such all ways.

00:54:29.930 --> 00:54:33.640
And Brenda was mentioning
about the college.

00:54:33.640 --> 00:54:36.010
I mean, the graduate school, I mean,

00:54:36.010 --> 00:54:38.923
it was kind of a peripheral thing here.

00:54:40.360 --> 00:54:43.910
I think, Rick and Levin and you know,

00:54:43.910 --> 00:54:47.030
it's helped during his tenure here

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:51.710
to make the entire university,
the place when you say Yale,

00:54:51.710 --> 00:54:56.490
it's not just the college,
the entire university,

00:54:56.490 --> 00:55:00.170
but all those things came about slowly

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:03.610
and so this was a very,
very dramatic change

00:55:03.610 --> 00:55:06.080
for an institution that
was kinda hidebound

00:55:06.080 --> 00:55:08.170
and looking mainly to the past.

00:55:08.170 --> 00:55:10.330
- Margaret, can I just
say one other thing,

00:55:10.330 --> 00:55:11.920
because I think it really is important.

00:55:11.920 --> 00:55:14.010
I think it's really important
to put this in the context.

00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:17.410
I mentioned earlier the public
school population doubled.

00:55:17.410 --> 00:55:19.700
The student of color population

00:55:19.700 --> 00:55:22.206
was doubling almost every year,

00:55:22.206 --> 00:55:24.590
for over a five year
period, and the Jewish

00:55:24.590 --> 00:55:27.730
and Catholic population during
that period doubled as well.

00:55:27.730 --> 00:55:31.650
So, Yale by the time you arrived

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:34.960
was a very, very different
place from the one

00:55:34.960 --> 00:55:38.563
that I went to from 1956 to 1960.

00:55:39.470 --> 00:55:41.013
- Did you wanna jump in Garry?

00:55:43.870 --> 00:55:44.884
- No.

00:55:44.884 --> 00:55:47.046
(audience laughs)

00:55:47.046 --> 00:55:50.780
- You always look so, you
know you look spellbound

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:52.770
and as if you're on the
verge of saying something,

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:55.080
so I'll put you on the
spot, I'm dying to ask.

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:58.410
I don't recall, so I'm gonna ask you,

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:01.720
did Bull Tales deal with co-education?

00:56:01.720 --> 00:56:05.000
To what degree was it
good grist for Bull Tales?

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.260
I tried to research this on
Google, but it was hard to find.

00:56:07.260 --> 00:56:09.556
- Well, I tried to research it too.

00:56:09.556 --> 00:56:10.790
(audience laughs)

00:56:10.790 --> 00:56:14.110
And I found, I published two collections

00:56:14.110 --> 00:56:16.140
of Bull Tales when I was a student,

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:19.790
and I dug out the second one.

00:56:19.790 --> 00:56:23.630
And the less we say
about that, the better,

00:56:23.630 --> 00:56:25.654
other than I'm so so sorry

00:56:25.654 --> 00:56:28.130
(audience laughs)

00:56:28.130 --> 00:56:31.770
For whatever small, toxic contribution

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:33.603
to your experience I made.

00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:38.950
What preceded that, when
I first started thinking

00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:40.450
about, well, what does this mean for us

00:56:40.450 --> 00:56:42.170
as a satirist on the Yale record,

00:56:42.170 --> 00:56:45.160
which is still is the humor magazine,

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:48.090
and we gathered here at
Yale a few weeks early

00:56:48.090 --> 00:56:52.750
to try to get out an early issue
about women coming to Yale,

00:56:52.750 --> 00:56:57.200
and how we enlisted the help of,

00:56:57.200 --> 00:57:01.130
a friend of my mother's who
was a professional model,

00:57:01.130 --> 00:57:03.480
runway model and she was like 5'11

00:57:03.480 --> 00:57:05.710
and had three feet of blonde hair.

00:57:05.710 --> 00:57:08.370
And thought, well this is a perfect avatar

00:57:08.370 --> 00:57:12.390
for the women that will
be arriving, we put her

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:15.930
on the cover as eye candy
with two words, the girls.

00:57:15.930 --> 00:57:19.452
And then the inside we had four profiles

00:57:19.452 --> 00:57:24.370
of supposedly incoming freshmen.

00:57:24.370 --> 00:57:26.130
Of course, we didn't know any

00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:30.430
but we, and we needed
pictures so we enlisted

00:57:30.430 --> 00:57:33.180
the help of our legacy girlfriends.

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:38.180
And who were all too happy to
make fun of the competition.

00:57:38.990 --> 00:57:43.000
So we took their pictures and
we wrote profiles for them.

00:57:43.000 --> 00:57:46.863
And, I can't imagine what
it would have been like,

00:57:46.863 --> 00:57:47.910
- [Margaret] You mean, you didn't--

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:50.857
- We're making fun of them
before we met any of them.

00:57:50.857 --> 00:57:53.480
- And were these real
women coming to Yale?

00:57:53.480 --> 00:57:56.885
- No, no, no, no, they
were just girls we knew.

00:57:56.885 --> 00:57:58.910
(audience laughs)
- So,

00:57:58.910 --> 00:58:00.270
- All babes, I imagine

00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:02.553
- Yes, so that was my opening move,

00:58:02.553 --> 00:58:06.190
and, going back and looking at some

00:58:06.190 --> 00:58:10.390
of those early strips that
the, there's no hiding it

00:58:10.390 --> 00:58:12.610
because it was in the the Welcome Center,

00:58:12.610 --> 00:58:16.460
there's a strip where
Mike arrives in college

00:58:16.460 --> 00:58:18.590
and finds that through some error,

00:58:18.590 --> 00:58:21.900
administrative error,
his roommate is a woman.

00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:24.670
That was, you think would set up

00:58:24.670 --> 00:58:26.563
some pretty interesting storylines,

00:58:28.470 --> 00:58:29.600
you would think would set up

00:58:29.600 --> 00:58:31.040
some pretty interesting storylines,

00:58:31.040 --> 00:58:34.337
but I think the less we
talk about where that went,

00:58:34.337 --> 00:58:36.260
(audience laughs)

00:58:36.260 --> 00:58:38.580
The better, I think the strip did become

00:58:38.580 --> 00:58:43.110
kind of a symbol of the old
order in some people's minds

00:58:43.110 --> 00:58:45.910
and I think, I was rewarded for that

00:58:45.910 --> 00:58:50.880
by being included in a book
called, "Women at Yale,"

00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:55.200
which was written by two
sociologists, grad students.

00:58:55.200 --> 00:58:58.520
Did you encounter that
Nancy in your research?

00:58:58.520 --> 00:59:01.930
It was written by two really interesting

00:59:01.930 --> 00:59:03.490
sociology grad students named

00:59:03.490 --> 00:59:05.750
Pepper Schwartz and Janet Lever.

00:59:05.750 --> 00:59:09.710
And they were into sort of
participatory fieldwork.

00:59:09.710 --> 00:59:11.690
And so they actually went out on dates,

00:59:11.690 --> 00:59:15.630
they went to mixers,
they went on road trips,

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:19.950
they all matter of humiliation
just to get a taste for it.

00:59:19.950 --> 00:59:23.530
And they interviewed a
lot of Yale students,

00:59:23.530 --> 00:59:27.880
including myself, and
I remember coming out

00:59:27.880 --> 00:59:30.410
of that interview, it
must have been in January,

00:59:30.410 --> 00:59:35.030
halfway through the year, and being

00:59:35.030 --> 00:59:37.670
very uncomfortable with my answers.

00:59:37.670 --> 00:59:39.180
They are the kind of
answers I would have given

00:59:39.180 --> 00:59:40.810
a year earlier, two years earlier,

00:59:40.810 --> 00:59:43.650
and probably answers
that were representative

00:59:43.650 --> 00:59:47.190
of a lot of Yale seniors at that time.

00:59:47.190 --> 00:59:50.530
And, I think and again,
you have to correct,

00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:51.973
correct me on the chronology but I think

00:59:51.973 --> 00:59:54.980
that there was a gathering, there was

00:59:54.980 --> 00:59:59.750
some women's gathering in
February, a conference.

00:59:59.750 --> 01:00:02.630
- I'm in the midst of
whatever time (mumbles)

01:00:02.630 --> 01:00:04.390
- There was this nation

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:08.910
of the alumni luncheon by the freshmen.

01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:10.360
- Right, the disruption
at well, at any rate,

01:00:10.360 --> 01:00:14.020
I, this is something that
I started thinking about.

01:00:14.020 --> 01:00:16.470
Remember the women's movement only arrived

01:00:16.470 --> 01:00:18.930
on campus as the undergraduate's did.

01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:22.580
I think if you would ask the
typical female undergraduate

01:00:22.580 --> 01:00:25.700
in the fall of 1969, are you a feminist?

01:00:25.700 --> 01:00:27.491
I think some of them would
have to think about it

01:00:27.491 --> 01:00:29.400
and some of them might not even know

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:32.080
what that meant as a belief system.

01:00:32.080 --> 01:00:35.580
If you would ask them, do
you think women should enjoy

01:00:35.580 --> 01:00:37.330
the same rights and opportunities as men?

01:00:37.330 --> 01:00:38.390
Well, that's a no brainer,

01:00:38.390 --> 01:00:41.340
that's why they were there,
that's how they got there.

01:00:41.340 --> 01:00:45.770
But the actual creation of a movement

01:00:45.770 --> 01:00:47.810
of women reaching out to one another

01:00:47.810 --> 01:00:49.740
had not happened, hadn't
even happen on the campus,

01:00:49.740 --> 01:00:52.620
it was hard to find women's
groups in the early.

01:00:52.620 --> 01:00:55.310
- Part of it was all the non freshmen,

01:00:55.310 --> 01:00:57.183
half of us didn't know one another

01:00:57.183 --> 01:01:01.000
because we were split up
in different colleges.

01:01:01.000 --> 01:01:04.660
So, that was, in other words,
we hadn't had the old campus,

01:01:04.660 --> 01:01:08.490
I guess bonding experience,
whatever you'd call it.

01:01:08.490 --> 01:01:10.050
Kurt, were you about to add something?

01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:13.060
- No, just that that was
a deliberate decision

01:01:13.060 --> 01:01:14.720
of the planning committee.

01:01:14.720 --> 01:01:18.033
- Oh, what was the deliberate
decision of the planning?

01:01:19.670 --> 01:01:23.470
- To make sure that women
were in all the colleges

01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:25.380
rather than in a single college

01:01:27.450 --> 01:01:29.450
for those who transferred as well

01:01:29.450 --> 01:01:31.380
as those who came in as freshmen.

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:33.200
- So in that, in the end,

01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:35.810
didn't Silliman have women, or no?

01:01:35.810 --> 01:01:37.130
There wasn't

01:01:37.130 --> 01:01:38.443
- They all did
- They all did.

01:01:38.443 --> 01:01:40.040
- Yes, yes.

01:01:40.040 --> 01:01:43.034
- There was a decision on
the part of the president

01:01:43.034 --> 01:01:47.340
to make Trumbull college,
a woman's college,

01:01:47.340 --> 01:01:50.740
and he appeared before
this Trumbull students

01:01:50.740 --> 01:01:53.957
who made sure that would not happen.

01:01:53.957 --> 01:01:55.671
(Margaret laughs)

01:01:55.671 --> 01:01:58.700
And it was understandable
because Kingman graduated

01:01:58.700 --> 01:02:03.700
in 1941, from 1941 to
1968 at that point, or 69,

01:02:04.357 --> 01:02:07.550
the residential college
system had come into its own.

01:02:07.550 --> 01:02:09.400
So people identified very strongly

01:02:09.400 --> 01:02:11.490
and in fact, I remember some of you,

01:02:11.490 --> 01:02:13.950
when you went to meetings
in the hockey rink,

01:02:13.950 --> 01:02:15.870
protesting one thing or another,

01:02:15.870 --> 01:02:18.660
you would introduce
yourself not with a surname,

01:02:18.660 --> 01:02:23.250
you'd be Mary from Silliman,
Hellen from Trumbull.

01:02:23.250 --> 01:02:28.250
So, the colleges had become
very strong identifying places

01:02:29.060 --> 01:02:31.140
for our undergraduates,
so when Kingman tried

01:02:31.140 --> 01:02:33.490
to take Trumbull away from the men,

01:02:33.490 --> 01:02:35.180
they would not let it happen.

01:02:36.080 --> 01:02:38.640
- So Brenda, talk a little bit more

01:02:38.640 --> 01:02:39.970
about what we're talking about here,

01:02:39.970 --> 01:02:43.341
the sort of social fabric,

01:02:43.341 --> 01:02:47.780
the community and the
way it was organized,

01:02:47.780 --> 01:02:50.500
because you probably had, am I right?

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:53.130
A lot more students
pouring out their heart

01:02:53.130 --> 01:02:55.483
or complaining than most would hear.

01:02:57.980 --> 01:03:01.720
- Not so much about social life.

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:05.180
Social life was like rooming
(mumbles)

01:03:05.180 --> 01:03:07.423
Things that we overlooked.

01:03:09.539 --> 01:03:12.342
There was a double next to
the Dean's apartment, but

01:03:12.342 --> 01:03:13.175
- [Margaret] There was a what?

01:03:13.175 --> 01:03:15.600
- A double room next to
the Dean's apartment,

01:03:15.600 --> 01:03:18.770
which actually turned into
like the honeymoon suite.

01:03:18.770 --> 01:03:20.260
I am proud to announce by the way

01:03:20.260 --> 01:03:23.250
that all those couples
who are still married,

01:03:23.250 --> 01:03:28.250
who occupied that double,
but no, I was not hearing,

01:03:29.060 --> 01:03:33.390
sort of, oh, I'm having problems socially.

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:36.540
I said the women just
were not complaining,

01:03:36.540 --> 01:03:38.300
the men were not complaining

01:03:38.300 --> 01:03:40.893
so much socially as academically.

01:03:41.918 --> 01:03:44.368
- [Margaret] (mumbles)
their academic complaints?

01:03:45.250 --> 01:03:46.520
- That they
- (mumbles) having women

01:03:46.520 --> 01:03:48.010
- That they weren't,
they weren't complaining

01:03:48.010 --> 01:03:49.550
about the women, they were complaining

01:03:49.550 --> 01:03:50.850
they couldn't do the work.

01:03:51.719 --> 01:03:53.740
(audience laughs)

01:03:53.740 --> 01:03:56.150
The women didn't complain
because they did the work.

01:03:56.150 --> 01:03:59.070
And you got to graduation, it was like,

01:03:59.070 --> 01:04:01.320
so and so woman summa cum laude,

01:04:01.320 --> 01:04:05.733
so and so guy barely squeaked by, etc.

01:04:07.140 --> 01:04:12.140
But, this was, I said I was so surprised

01:04:12.490 --> 01:04:15.060
that was that the women got along.

01:04:15.060 --> 01:04:18.187
I said you just wouldn't have believed.

01:04:18.187 --> 01:04:19.683
- [Margaret] And why did you
think that was surprising?

01:04:19.683 --> 01:04:24.550
- Because they were so different
in terms of personality.

01:04:24.550 --> 01:04:26.850
I mean, you would not think that they

01:04:26.850 --> 01:04:30.400
could actually socialize, they
could have lunch together.

01:04:30.400 --> 01:04:32.660
I mean, they live next to each other,

01:04:32.660 --> 01:04:33.760
well, wouldn't that be enough?

01:04:33.760 --> 01:04:36.730
I mean, if you really didn't
like that one so much, but no,

01:04:36.730 --> 01:04:39.410
they seem to be
- Find other (mumbles)

01:04:39.410 --> 01:04:42.023
- They made little groups cohorts,

01:04:43.414 --> 01:04:45.457
and I guess felt comfortable that way.

01:04:47.663 --> 01:04:49.380
- [Margaret] So one
change to the social life,

01:04:49.380 --> 01:04:52.730
of course, was dating and the road trip

01:04:52.730 --> 01:04:55.690
and I'm gonna ask Garry and
Kurt to describe Berlin.

01:04:55.690 --> 01:04:58.125
- I never went on a road trip while I was

01:04:58.125 --> 01:05:01.011
(audience laughs)

01:05:01.011 --> 01:05:02.470
ever

01:05:02.470 --> 01:05:04.104
- Garry.
- I did.

01:05:04.104 --> 01:05:06.560
(audience laughs)

01:05:06.560 --> 01:05:08.740
Would even hitchhike to Smith

01:05:08.740 --> 01:05:12.429
and those early mixers that I went to, but

01:05:12.429 --> 01:05:14.110
(audience mumbles)

01:05:14.110 --> 01:05:16.980
- He's confessing that
he did go on road trip.

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:19.500
- I did, those that, there were mixers

01:05:19.500 --> 01:05:23.270
where busloads of young
women were brought to Yale.

01:05:23.270 --> 01:05:25.580
And, but there were also the mixers

01:05:25.580 --> 01:05:28.770
to which we were invited
at women's campuses.

01:05:28.770 --> 01:05:31.670
And, no it was a horror show (laughs)

01:05:31.670 --> 01:05:32.503
- [Margaret] It was a what?

01:05:32.503 --> 01:05:33.570
- It was a horror show, I mean,

01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:35.460
there's no other way to describe it.

01:05:35.460 --> 01:05:40.400
And I was very shy and I was an art guy.

01:05:42.670 --> 01:05:44.653
There's I think, you know, there's,

01:05:46.047 --> 01:05:47.430
one of the things that Yale did for me

01:05:47.430 --> 01:05:49.573
was that it made it okay to be an art guy.

01:05:51.140 --> 01:05:53.160
It's no accident that women have been

01:05:53.160 --> 01:05:55.120
at the Art School for 150 years.

01:05:55.120 --> 01:06:00.120
I think that art as a
discipline was seen as soft,

01:06:01.020 --> 01:06:05.360
unserious, and, but it
allowed me to create

01:06:05.360 --> 01:06:07.490
a connection with the women friends

01:06:07.490 --> 01:06:11.670
that I made subsequently in
my sophomore junior year.

01:06:11.670 --> 01:06:16.670
And, it was something that
you could find support for

01:06:19.140 --> 01:06:20.390
once you got to Yale, because I've been

01:06:20.390 --> 01:06:25.310
through all these seven years
of macho prep school culture.

01:06:25.310 --> 01:06:27.590
So it was a bit of a relief.

01:06:27.590 --> 01:06:29.380
- So, I think now we're
going to open it up

01:06:29.380 --> 01:06:32.330
to questions from the audience and,

01:06:32.330 --> 01:06:34.493
or is someone moving around with the mic?

01:06:36.770 --> 01:06:37.603
- Yeah, so

01:06:37.603 --> 01:06:39.860
- Marg, can I ask Nancy, though.

01:06:39.860 --> 01:06:43.920
In your experience, the
experience of women at Princeton

01:06:43.920 --> 01:06:47.100
was a very different than the experience

01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:49.823
of women at Yale from just your study.

01:06:50.802 --> 01:06:54.810
- Yeah, it, and I say
this not as a chauvinist

01:06:54.810 --> 01:06:59.570
for Princeton, but it
went somewhat better.

01:06:59.570 --> 01:07:03.060
It didn't go better in all the ways

01:07:03.060 --> 01:07:05.250
that it didn't go well at Yale,

01:07:05.250 --> 01:07:08.440
which is to say you're
the only woman in a class

01:07:08.440 --> 01:07:13.440
and the instructor asks you
for the woman's point of view.

01:07:13.630 --> 01:07:15.540
In literature or psychology, maybe,

01:07:15.540 --> 01:07:20.540
but in math and physics,
it didn't go better

01:07:20.930 --> 01:07:25.930
in the sense that, a woman
walks into a study room

01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:30.940
in the library and the
large number of men there

01:07:30.940 --> 01:07:35.100
start giggling and she
leaves and never goes back.

01:07:35.100 --> 01:07:38.545
Again, it didn't go better in

01:07:38.545 --> 01:07:40.430
- So you're saying these
things did happen at Princeton.

01:07:40.430 --> 01:07:42.300
- Absolutely, though all of that

01:07:42.300 --> 01:07:44.320
sort of thing happened at Princeton.

01:07:44.320 --> 01:07:46.980
All of the unease of the women

01:07:46.980 --> 01:07:49.700
when their great friend
during the week brings

01:07:49.700 --> 01:07:53.270
a great friend from Smith
or Wellesley on the weekend,

01:07:53.270 --> 01:07:55.870
all of that was descriptive of Princeton.

01:07:55.870 --> 01:08:00.100
The ways in which it
went better are several.

01:08:00.100 --> 01:08:03.733
First of all, Princeton had 170 women

01:08:08.780 --> 01:08:12.470
in the first year, Yale had 576.

01:08:12.470 --> 01:08:16.710
Now, you might say, just 170 women.

01:08:16.710 --> 01:08:19.060
What that allowed was, first of all,

01:08:19.060 --> 01:08:22.690
we had the housing to
accommodate the women

01:08:22.690 --> 01:08:27.690
and then we quite quickly
acquired new living spaces.

01:08:28.080 --> 01:08:30.640
We turned the Princeton in the hotel

01:08:30.640 --> 01:08:34.430
into a dormitory residential college,

01:08:34.430 --> 01:08:39.430
we built new dormitories
so that intense crunch

01:08:39.440 --> 01:08:42.367
on housing space here were men and women

01:08:42.367 --> 01:08:46.920
who were so overcrowded in rooms meant

01:08:46.920 --> 01:08:51.920
for fewer students was
not the case at Princeton.

01:08:52.720 --> 01:08:55.860
It went better at Princeton, in that

01:08:55.860 --> 01:09:00.430
the woman hired as an
assistant dean of students

01:09:00.430 --> 01:09:04.940
to work on the development
of co-education,

01:09:04.940 --> 01:09:07.930
the acculturation of the women students,

01:09:07.930 --> 01:09:12.210
was highly respected by
the president, the provost,

01:09:12.210 --> 01:09:15.620
the Dean of the College,
the Dean of Students

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:20.120
who listened to her, who
acted on her recommendations,

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:25.120
none of the marginalization
that Elga Wasserman experienced.

01:09:26.700 --> 01:09:31.230
And it went better at Princeton,

01:09:31.230 --> 01:09:33.600
in the sense that this woman dean

01:09:33.600 --> 01:09:36.380
was able to get to know personally,

01:09:36.380 --> 01:09:40.350
every single one of
those 170 women students

01:09:40.350 --> 01:09:44.020
to listen to their stories and experiences

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:48.110
and then to formulate
institutional responses

01:09:48.110 --> 01:09:53.110
to improve the situation
as smart and effective

01:09:53.320 --> 01:09:55.010
as Elga Wasserman was.

01:09:55.010 --> 01:10:00.010
She couldn't get to know
576 students personally

01:10:00.120 --> 01:10:03.320
and as has been said,
certainly the President

01:10:03.320 --> 01:10:07.890
was not listening to her recommendations.

01:10:07.890 --> 01:10:11.400
So those are some of the
ways in which I think

01:10:11.400 --> 01:10:15.150
it was easier at Princeton,
which is pretty paradoxical,

01:10:15.150 --> 01:10:18.360
there's nothing in the
history and traditions

01:10:18.360 --> 01:10:21.200
of that place that would
lead you to believe

01:10:21.200 --> 01:10:24.053
that co-education would have gone well.

01:10:24.053 --> 01:10:27.280
(audience laughs)

01:10:27.280 --> 01:10:30.236
- I remind you that
Nancy's still at Princeton.

01:10:30.236 --> 01:10:31.210
(audience laughs)

01:10:31.210 --> 01:10:34.320
I mean, just so you know
the institution well.

01:10:34.320 --> 01:10:36.990
So, how are we doing questions here?

01:10:36.990 --> 01:10:39.440
All right, I think, go right here.

01:10:39.440 --> 01:10:43.820
And if you just introduce
yourself and your year.

01:10:43.820 --> 01:10:46.710
- I'm Kelsey Kauffman and
I graduated in the 71.

01:10:46.710 --> 01:10:49.170
And so I love the
opportunity of celebrating

01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:53.260
the past as a chance to think
about discrimination today.

01:10:53.260 --> 01:10:55.270
There are hundreds of
thousands of our sisters

01:10:55.270 --> 01:10:57.110
who are not welcome here at Yale,

01:10:57.110 --> 01:10:58.990
and those are post incarcerated women.

01:10:58.990 --> 01:11:01.700
A number of years ago, I
started the college program

01:11:01.700 --> 01:11:03.410
at the Indiana Women's Prison, ran it

01:11:03.410 --> 01:11:05.050
for quite a number of years,

01:11:05.050 --> 01:11:07.260
still work with my former students.

01:11:07.260 --> 01:11:09.920
When one of my outstanding
students applied here

01:11:09.920 --> 01:11:12.173
to Yale University for graduate school,

01:11:13.160 --> 01:11:14.530
her application was not considered.

01:11:14.530 --> 01:11:15.650
- [Margaret] So, I meant to say,

01:11:15.650 --> 01:11:16.760
do you have a question?

01:11:16.760 --> 01:11:17.900
- Yeah, okay.

01:11:17.900 --> 01:11:21.100
So, Kurt schmoke,
- Yes, ma'am

01:11:21.100 --> 01:11:24.150
- I would love to hear
you speak about parallels

01:11:24.150 --> 01:11:28.800
between discrimination against
post incarcerated people now,

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:33.410
compared to discrimination
against women back in our era.

01:11:33.410 --> 01:11:34.873
- Just real quick, though.

01:11:36.830 --> 01:11:40.580
My current job as a president
University of Baltimore,

01:11:40.580 --> 01:11:44.530
we actually have a degree granting program

01:11:44.530 --> 01:11:47.640
into Jessup Correctional Institution,

01:11:47.640 --> 01:11:49.840
right outside the city.

01:11:49.840 --> 01:11:53.700
And so, we had to take young men

01:11:53.700 --> 01:11:56.880
who are in a, within
three years of release

01:11:56.880 --> 01:12:00.850
and they are admitted
to our academic programs

01:12:00.850 --> 01:12:03.080
and then when they release, they come on

01:12:03.080 --> 01:12:05.900
to our campus to complete their degree.

01:12:05.900 --> 01:12:08.910
So, we are reaching out
to returning citizens

01:12:08.910 --> 01:12:11.500
and we do have a number of our professors

01:12:11.500 --> 01:12:12.950
who are also involved with women

01:12:12.950 --> 01:12:14.900
and who are incarcerated now.

01:12:14.900 --> 01:12:17.760
So I can't speak for
Yale, but I don't think

01:12:17.760 --> 01:12:22.680
that that should be a
barrier to admissions.

01:12:22.680 --> 01:12:27.650
And I'm sure that other
alumni would support

01:12:27.650 --> 01:12:30.527
making sure that that
barrier doesn't exist.

01:12:30.527 --> 01:12:33.970
(audience claps)

01:12:33.970 --> 01:12:37.867
- Another question, yes,
right there, just you.

01:12:43.960 --> 01:12:48.663
- I'm Leah Greenwald, I
am from the class of 72.

01:12:50.020 --> 01:12:53.750
Could you, where's Avi Soifer?

01:12:53.750 --> 01:12:55.060
Could you
- Avi?

01:12:55.060 --> 01:12:56.270
- Where's Avi Soifer?

01:12:56.270 --> 01:12:58.271
I mean, my understanding.

01:12:58.271 --> 01:12:59.470
(mumbles)

01:12:59.470 --> 01:13:00.303
- [Kurt] He's in Hawaii,

01:13:00.303 --> 01:13:02.710
he's the Dean of the Law School in Hawaii.

01:13:02.710 --> 01:13:05.510
- Right, but right this minute,

01:13:05.510 --> 01:13:07.690
I thought he was even gonna be here,

01:13:07.690 --> 01:13:12.690
but my understanding from friends of mine

01:13:12.920 --> 01:13:16.550
in my class who were
freshmen without women

01:13:17.970 --> 01:13:20.690
was that Avi did it all.

01:13:20.690 --> 01:13:24.197
And so, now you know,
that's the impression

01:13:24.197 --> 01:13:26.340
of an undergraduate at the time,

01:13:26.340 --> 01:13:31.123
but perhaps you could add his role,

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:33.960
some information about his role

01:13:33.960 --> 01:13:38.960
in the lead up to the
announcement that okay,

01:13:40.020 --> 01:13:41.993
women come on in, thanks.

01:13:43.260 --> 01:13:45.800
- Kurt
- Well, Avi Soifer

01:13:45.800 --> 01:13:50.800
and Derek Sher did it
all, and in fact (mumbles)

01:13:51.387 --> 01:13:52.220
- Can you just explain in
a nutshell who they were.

01:13:53.820 --> 01:13:56.340
- Derek Sher and Avi Soifer were the two

01:13:56.340 --> 01:14:01.118
who organized the co-education week.

01:14:01.118 --> 01:14:04.910
And Avi also was a strong, strong advocate

01:14:04.910 --> 01:14:06.570
for co education, he probably was

01:14:06.570 --> 01:14:09.910
the most outspoken
advocate for co-education.

01:14:09.910 --> 01:14:13.493
And I'm sorry, he's not here
because you have a lot to say.

01:14:13.493 --> 01:14:16.583
- And in Anne's book in particular,

01:14:17.630 --> 01:14:21.520
there's a great, a lot of
information about Avi there.

01:14:21.520 --> 01:14:25.840
I don't know, whether
he could have been here,

01:14:25.840 --> 01:14:28.597
but if you'd like to know
you're absolutely right,

01:14:28.597 --> 01:14:31.120
he was a leader in this effort,

01:14:31.120 --> 01:14:33.650
and I think is captured
pretty well in here.

01:14:33.650 --> 01:14:34.483
- Right (mumbles)
- I just wanna say

01:14:34.483 --> 01:14:38.010
for a moment, Avi was
invited, he sent his regrets.

01:14:38.010 --> 01:14:40.183
He invited, I'm not sure
he may not regret this

01:14:40.183 --> 01:14:42.510
because there are, how
many people in the audience

01:14:42.510 --> 01:14:44.360
but he said, If any of
you wanna contact him,

01:14:44.360 --> 01:14:45.610
he'd be glad to hear from you.

01:14:45.610 --> 01:14:47.080
So,
(audience laughs)

01:14:47.080 --> 01:14:49.960
But he was invited and
we're sorry he's not here.

01:14:49.960 --> 01:14:51.023
- Thank you, Eve.

01:14:51.920 --> 01:14:54.900
The lady in the striped shirt, I think.

01:14:54.900 --> 01:14:56.883
No, (mumbles) I'm sorry, I meant you.

01:15:00.540 --> 01:15:02.970
- Thank you, My name is Maggie Coon,

01:15:02.970 --> 01:15:06.530
class of 1973, Calhoun Grace Hopper.

01:15:06.530 --> 01:15:09.380
I'm hoping Anne Perkins
is in the audience.

01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:10.770
Is she here?

01:15:10.770 --> 01:15:14.960
Kind of as a follow up, I
had the distinct impression

01:15:14.960 --> 01:15:18.990
from reading your book, that
Kingman Brewster displayed

01:15:18.990 --> 01:15:22.860
a lot of resistance to co-education,

01:15:22.860 --> 01:15:25.610
and so, I'd love to hear a little bit more

01:15:25.610 --> 01:15:28.360
of your reflection on
that because I sort of got

01:15:28.360 --> 01:15:32.180
a little bit different
impression from our panelists,

01:15:32.180 --> 01:15:34.743
if you're willing to comment.

01:15:43.100 --> 01:15:43.933
- Sure.

01:15:46.647 --> 01:15:49.163
(mumbles)

01:15:49.163 --> 01:15:51.496
I'm standing
- You're so shy.

01:15:52.830 --> 01:15:55.700
- The woman
(audience claps)

01:15:58.533 --> 01:16:00.370
I never met Kingman Brewster,

01:16:00.370 --> 01:16:04.170
he left before I came here as a freshman.

01:16:04.170 --> 01:16:09.170
But in reading about him, I
was so struck and listening

01:16:09.560 --> 01:16:12.720
to those of you whom I
interviewed about him,

01:16:12.720 --> 01:16:16.060
so struck by the admiration
students had for him.

01:16:16.060 --> 01:16:17.930
His stands on the Vietnam War,

01:16:17.930 --> 01:16:21.220
which were incredibly courageous.

01:16:21.220 --> 01:16:23.600
And I think I see them a
little differently than you,

01:16:23.600 --> 01:16:25.940
Nancy, and that I think
they were very thoughtful.

01:16:25.940 --> 01:16:29.230
He would go off and write
his president's report

01:16:29.230 --> 01:16:31.940
on Marta's vineyard,
and really think about

01:16:31.940 --> 01:16:35.960
the place of a university in
welcoming students of color

01:16:35.960 --> 01:16:39.780
who had not been included
at a place like Yale before,

01:16:39.780 --> 01:16:43.580
in taking stands on
issues such as Vietnam,

01:16:43.580 --> 01:16:46.470
but I do think there was a blind spot

01:16:46.470 --> 01:16:48.450
when it came to women and for me,

01:16:48.450 --> 01:16:53.200
the answer really lies in
the incredible insularity

01:16:53.200 --> 01:16:55.000
of many of the men at Yale.

01:16:55.000 --> 01:16:58.000
So Garry has talked about how he had been

01:16:58.000 --> 01:17:00.480
at an all-male school for 11 years.

01:17:00.480 --> 01:17:04.200
So Brewster went from all-male prep school

01:17:04.200 --> 01:17:08.203
to all-male Yale to nearly all-male,

01:17:09.120 --> 01:17:11.530
Harvard Law School, back to Yale,

01:17:11.530 --> 01:17:15.880
where he appointed all
of his closest advisors,

01:17:15.880 --> 01:17:19.380
as men who had gone to all-male Yale,

01:17:19.380 --> 01:17:23.140
and the trustees were men who
had gone to all-male Yale.

01:17:23.140 --> 01:17:25.840
So there's no, and he marries a woman

01:17:25.840 --> 01:17:30.070
whose father went to all-male
Yale, and she went to Vassar.

01:17:30.070 --> 01:17:35.070
So there's not an ability to
see beyond that world of men.

01:17:35.760 --> 01:17:38.410
And I do think that that hampers him,

01:17:38.410 --> 01:17:42.050
and particularly this
idea that Yale needed

01:17:42.050 --> 01:17:44.460
to have a quota that
hasn't come up yet today,

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:48.120
but Yale needed to have a
quota on the number of women

01:17:48.120 --> 01:17:51.380
because Yale's job is to produce leaders

01:17:51.380 --> 01:17:56.380
and, 1000 male leaders and
because men are leaders

01:17:57.250 --> 01:17:59.510
and women are not, or
so the reasoning went,

01:17:59.510 --> 01:18:03.100
why spend any spaces that
you didn't have to on women?

01:18:03.100 --> 01:18:06.910
I love that, is an
example of the ancillary

01:18:06.910 --> 01:18:09.420
because if he had just
looked out his window

01:18:09.420 --> 01:18:11.690
in Woodbridge Hall, he
might have been able

01:18:11.690 --> 01:18:13.880
to see down to the law school,

01:18:13.880 --> 01:18:16.190
where Marian Wright Edelman had graduated

01:18:16.190 --> 01:18:18.950
a number of years before,
where Hillary Rodham

01:18:18.950 --> 01:18:21.040
had just started as a student,

01:18:21.040 --> 01:18:22.500
or down to the graduate school

01:18:22.500 --> 01:18:24.750
where Janet Yellen was getting her degree.

01:18:24.750 --> 01:18:27.490
So there were leaders at Yale,

01:18:27.490 --> 01:18:31.280
even when women leaders at
Yale, even when you arrived,

01:18:31.280 --> 01:18:35.617
but I think that was hard for
some of them and (mumbles)

01:18:35.617 --> 01:18:37.467
(audience claps)
- Very interesting.

01:18:40.806 --> 01:18:42.890
I wanna get someone from there, but first,

01:18:42.890 --> 01:18:45.963
yes, that with the, sort of Karl scarf.

01:18:50.190 --> 01:18:54.190
- Hi, I'm Sarah Coffin and
I was in the class of 73.

01:18:54.190 --> 01:18:56.470
And I wanted to address
this point a little bit

01:18:56.470 --> 01:18:58.710
on personal experience and ask the panel.

01:18:58.710 --> 01:19:02.350
Two, I had personal
experience when I came here

01:19:02.350 --> 01:19:04.430
and went around with my brother on a tour

01:19:04.430 --> 01:19:08.310
given by in (mumbles) and I asked him

01:19:08.310 --> 01:19:11.777
at the time it was 68, I said,

01:19:11.777 --> 01:19:14.580
"Oh, this is perfect, I
really wanna go here."

01:19:14.580 --> 01:19:19.137
And he said, "I have the
support of the President,

01:19:19.137 --> 01:19:22.767
"and that's why I'm saying
to me, we will go coed,

01:19:22.767 --> 01:19:26.457
"I don't know, I don't
think we're gonna be able

01:19:26.457 --> 01:19:27.840
"to do it in time for you."

01:19:27.840 --> 01:19:30.140
And then of course, things got sped up.

01:19:30.140 --> 01:19:32.860
But he said Kingman is for it,

01:19:32.860 --> 01:19:37.090
and we just need a little more time.

01:19:37.090 --> 01:19:40.670
So, to address that, I had this word

01:19:40.670 --> 01:19:44.410
from the Director of Admissions
as a kid going around,

01:19:44.410 --> 01:19:47.300
that he was saying he had
the support of the President.

01:19:47.300 --> 01:19:50.010
But to the question I
wanted to ask the panel

01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:53.930
is maybe with those of
you who had experience

01:19:53.930 --> 01:19:58.000
of the subsequent Yale after we were here,

01:19:58.000 --> 01:20:00.290
my impression has been that,

01:20:00.290 --> 01:20:03.760
a lot of the dissatisfaction
has been expressed

01:20:03.760 --> 01:20:07.270
by students who came after
us, who expected Yale

01:20:07.270 --> 01:20:11.060
to be completely ready for
women when they still weren't.

01:20:11.060 --> 01:20:13.320
And maybe this is 10 years out,

01:20:13.320 --> 01:20:14.750
or maybe even a little less.

01:20:14.750 --> 01:20:18.440
And at least from my own
personal experience, I arrived.

01:20:18.440 --> 01:20:20.090
It was crazy, in fact, we all went

01:20:20.090 --> 01:20:21.580
into the bathroom the first time,

01:20:21.580 --> 01:20:23.560
and there were all these multiple colors,

01:20:23.560 --> 01:20:25.470
and they were urinals, and we said, great,

01:20:25.470 --> 01:20:28.530
that's their way of making
the bathrooms for women.

01:20:28.530 --> 01:20:32.607
And it was funny, and we showed up

01:20:32.607 --> 01:20:34.560
and at least I didn't particularly care

01:20:34.560 --> 01:20:36.810
if I was the only girl,
and I guess we all knew

01:20:36.810 --> 01:20:39.220
we were in for an experiment,
I'm sure other people

01:20:39.220 --> 01:20:42.290
had terrible moments, and
I'm not saying they didn't,

01:20:42.290 --> 01:20:44.160
but I'm wondering if those of you

01:20:44.160 --> 01:20:46.340
who've been involved with the university

01:20:46.340 --> 01:20:48.910
as I have in the arts, I have found

01:20:48.910 --> 01:20:52.170
that there's dissatisfaction and the sound

01:20:52.170 --> 01:20:55.207
as if things were worse
at Yale than at Princeton.

01:20:55.207 --> 01:20:57.360
And I know the tennis team went down there

01:20:57.360 --> 01:20:59.970
and they were just, attacked

01:20:59.970 --> 01:21:01.373
by the men overnight in the (mumbles)

01:21:01.373 --> 01:21:03.417
- [Margaret] I'm sorry but
we can't quite hear you.

01:21:03.417 --> 01:21:06.850
- No, so I'm saying that,
what is your impression

01:21:06.850 --> 01:21:09.130
about whether a lot of
this dissatisfaction

01:21:09.130 --> 01:21:11.790
that's expressed about the early time

01:21:11.790 --> 01:21:16.203
is in fact, retro thinking
about interviewing women later?

01:21:18.000 --> 01:21:18.950
- Can I try something?

01:21:18.950 --> 01:21:22.353
I have a daughter who
graduated the class of 1986,

01:21:22.353 --> 01:21:23.860
and a daughter-in-law who got her PhD here

01:21:23.860 --> 01:21:28.010
in the year 2000, and I have to say

01:21:28.010 --> 01:21:32.140
that both of them loved
their Yale experience,

01:21:32.140 --> 01:21:33.700
they felt totally accepted.

01:21:33.700 --> 01:21:36.943
My daughter then went on
to the architecture school.

01:21:38.510 --> 01:21:42.423
15 years later, Yale was
truly co-educational.

01:21:43.970 --> 01:21:46.349
- But I share with you as one of the,

01:21:46.349 --> 01:21:48.830
I was on the class of
71, there was a sense

01:21:48.830 --> 01:21:51.616
that this was an adventure, and

01:21:51.616 --> 01:21:54.366
(audience claps)

01:21:56.337 --> 01:21:59.040
And so maybe we didn't
have the expectations

01:21:59.040 --> 01:22:02.610
that someone even coming along
five years later would have,

01:22:02.610 --> 01:22:07.610
where women felt, like they
been added on, tacked on.

01:22:08.140 --> 01:22:09.413
- Ah, yes, right here.

01:22:15.080 --> 01:22:18.100
- Hi, I'm Marcia Eckard, class of 71 J

01:22:19.550 --> 01:22:24.423
and, I don't, this is my
question to everybody.

01:22:25.800 --> 01:22:27.160
I've been getting the impression

01:22:27.160 --> 01:22:28.820
and it's perhaps it's because

01:22:28.820 --> 01:22:33.550
the class of 73 started out at Vanderbilt.

01:22:33.550 --> 01:22:36.390
And, the rest of us were sort of,

01:22:36.390 --> 01:22:38.010
I sort of have this
image that they took us

01:22:38.010 --> 01:22:39.926
like seeds and just threw us.

01:22:39.926 --> 01:22:41.550
(audience laughs)

01:22:41.550 --> 01:22:46.280
If you had a roommate, who was a friend,

01:22:46.280 --> 01:22:48.470
you did develop a woman friend,

01:22:48.470 --> 01:22:50.620
my roommate was a White Black Panther.

01:22:50.620 --> 01:22:52.610
So she sort of went off.

01:22:52.610 --> 01:22:55.440
And, so I have the feeling that

01:22:55.440 --> 01:22:59.160
there was more cohesiveness
in the class of 73.

01:22:59.160 --> 01:23:02.960
I see here of a number of
things that they did together,

01:23:02.960 --> 01:23:05.620
whereas frankly, I didn't really know

01:23:05.620 --> 01:23:08.750
other (mumbles)
- (mumbles) okay

01:23:08.750 --> 01:23:11.860
- So I just wondered is
that anybody's impression

01:23:11.860 --> 01:23:15.840
that the class of 73 had slightly more,

01:23:15.840 --> 01:23:18.743
maybe not a lot, but slightly
more experience together?

01:23:21.905 --> 01:23:24.822
(audience mumbles)

01:23:27.707 --> 01:23:28.910
- [Margaret] Let's go to one person

01:23:28.910 --> 01:23:30.290
who is gonna answer this.

01:23:30.290 --> 01:23:33.613
- I'm Emily Fine, class of 73 JE.

01:23:35.480 --> 01:23:37.710
And yes, I think definitely there was

01:23:37.710 --> 01:23:41.870
a Vanderbilt cult that
kept us somewhat sane,

01:23:41.870 --> 01:23:44.830
but remember, all of you
talk about experiment

01:23:44.830 --> 01:23:46.130
and how exciting it was, and yes,

01:23:46.130 --> 01:23:48.493
indeed it was, but we were 17 and 18.

01:23:49.755 --> 01:23:53.560
And, is it really fair to experiment

01:23:53.560 --> 01:23:55.245
on 17, 18 year old girls?

01:23:55.245 --> 01:23:56.870
(audience laughs)

01:23:56.870 --> 01:24:00.573
So that's remark number one,
sorry, it's not a question.

01:24:01.970 --> 01:24:05.570
Question number two is, this morning,

01:24:05.570 --> 01:24:10.500
to me is in some ways a metaphor for 69.

01:24:10.500 --> 01:24:14.000
You are having hundreds of women sit here

01:24:14.000 --> 01:24:17.648
for three hours with
smaller bladders than men.

01:24:17.648 --> 01:24:20.050
(audience laughs)

01:24:20.050 --> 01:24:22.570
And whoever arranged this, I love you Eve,

01:24:22.570 --> 01:24:24.460
did not arrange a bathroom check

01:24:24.460 --> 01:24:26.800
unless you're all on a
(mumbles), but I'm not.

01:24:26.800 --> 01:24:29.420
So anyway, just that was
just sort of a funny comment.

01:24:29.420 --> 01:24:31.530
I sort of said, aha, here we are,

01:24:31.530 --> 01:24:33.760
not enough bathrooms for women in 69

01:24:33.760 --> 01:24:36.350
and not enough bathroom breaks in today.

01:24:36.350 --> 01:24:37.963
But okay, that was just
- (mumbles)

01:24:37.963 --> 01:24:39.293
- A sort of observation.

01:24:40.540 --> 01:24:43.810
But I would like to say
- Okay, so over here (mumbles)

01:24:43.810 --> 01:24:45.290
- But I would like to comment about

01:24:45.290 --> 01:24:47.210
this is a question for the panel.

01:24:47.210 --> 01:24:49.090
So one of the things that's happened to me

01:24:49.090 --> 01:24:51.480
as a New Haven resident all these years,

01:24:51.480 --> 01:24:53.620
is that people come up to me and say,

01:24:53.620 --> 01:24:56.440
now that there are so many women at Yale,

01:24:56.440 --> 01:24:57.780
there certainly can't be a need

01:24:57.780 --> 01:25:00.180
for an office of women anymore, right?

01:25:00.180 --> 01:25:02.710
And so my question is,

01:25:02.710 --> 01:25:04.930
even though numbers may have increased,

01:25:04.930 --> 01:25:08.220
I still feel that, just
like I felt back then,

01:25:08.220 --> 01:25:11.560
that although there was
excitement about us coming,

01:25:11.560 --> 01:25:14.650
there really wasn't the
support in terms of mentoring,

01:25:14.650 --> 01:25:16.650
there was no, I didn't feel
like anyone reached out

01:25:16.650 --> 01:25:19.080
to see if I was surviving, I feel

01:25:19.080 --> 01:25:20.710
that even though women maybe in fact,

01:25:20.710 --> 01:25:23.390
in the numbers, majority now, I don't know

01:25:23.390 --> 01:25:25.660
that there's still so
many specific issues,

01:25:25.660 --> 01:25:29.140
as we all know, about
being female on campus

01:25:29.140 --> 01:25:31.740
and still a somewhat
male dominated society.

01:25:31.740 --> 01:25:36.290
And I just feel like, as
the trailblazers here,

01:25:36.290 --> 01:25:38.760
it is our responsibility
to still stay in touch

01:25:38.760 --> 01:25:41.820
with a women's experiment
- Thank you so much

01:25:41.820 --> 01:25:43.410
I'm gonna go to, we have spend,

01:25:43.410 --> 01:25:44.800
so many people with their hands up

01:25:44.800 --> 01:25:46.920
and we only have 15 minutes.

01:25:48.443 --> 01:25:49.860
- [Susan] Can I go?

01:25:49.860 --> 01:25:50.693
- [Margaret] No, no (mumbles)

01:25:50.693 --> 01:25:52.040
- [Susan] I just
- I have ignored--

01:25:52.040 --> 01:25:52.873
- May I please?

01:25:53.730 --> 01:25:55.600
One more question?

01:25:55.600 --> 01:25:59.530
I'm Susan (mumbles), 1971 Calhoun.

01:25:59.530 --> 01:26:01.410
I knew president Peter
when he was spotting

01:26:01.410 --> 01:26:03.650
a very nice mustache, and hanging out

01:26:03.650 --> 01:26:07.060
at the grad professional
center, way back when.

01:26:07.060 --> 01:26:11.343
I also came back to Yale
and got my PhD, much later.

01:26:12.400 --> 01:26:13.860
One of the things that struck me,

01:26:13.860 --> 01:26:17.920
my first came to Yale was
the lack of women faculty.

01:26:17.920 --> 01:26:19.750
I didn't realize at the time

01:26:19.750 --> 01:26:23.140
that overall nationally only one to 2%

01:26:23.140 --> 01:26:26.210
of faculty members were any university,

01:26:26.210 --> 01:26:27.850
national average or women,

01:26:27.850 --> 01:26:29.720
but at Yale it must have been point one

01:26:29.720 --> 01:26:31.160
or something like that.

01:26:31.160 --> 01:26:34.080
Coming from Barnard, it was a shock.

01:26:34.080 --> 01:26:37.760
And I wonder whether any thought went

01:26:37.760 --> 01:26:41.360
into increasing the
number of women faculty

01:26:41.360 --> 01:26:44.610
to develop better parity with male faculty

01:26:44.610 --> 01:26:47.970
as a part of co-education,
and a larger question

01:26:47.970 --> 01:26:50.610
looming over our get together.

01:26:50.610 --> 01:26:52.840
I remember hearing back then when I asked

01:26:52.840 --> 01:26:55.470
about why there were no women faculty,

01:26:55.470 --> 01:26:58.280
the answer was well, they
couldn't find enough women

01:26:58.280 --> 01:27:01.860
of quality, of that level at the time,

01:27:01.860 --> 01:27:04.510
to become faculty members at Yale.

01:27:04.510 --> 01:27:07.970
And now here we are, 50 years later,

01:27:07.970 --> 01:27:10.940
and although 50% or maybe a bit more

01:27:10.940 --> 01:27:14.040
of the student population are women,

01:27:14.040 --> 01:27:18.710
we have come nowhere near
that number in women faculty.

01:27:18.710 --> 01:27:20.710
So going back to the beginning,

01:27:20.710 --> 01:27:25.070
was any thought ever giving
to developing women faculty

01:27:25.070 --> 01:27:27.580
or future faculty members at Yale?

01:27:27.580 --> 01:27:29.131
Thank you.

01:27:29.131 --> 01:27:31.881
(audience claps)

01:27:33.474 --> 01:27:34.750
(mumbles)

01:27:34.750 --> 01:27:37.400
- John, I'm handing
this hot potato to you.

01:27:37.400 --> 01:27:41.693
- Yeah, yeah, but that
wasn't my job description.

01:27:41.693 --> 01:27:43.100
(audience laughs)

01:27:43.100 --> 01:27:44.950
But, there was a lot of talk,

01:27:44.950 --> 01:27:46.720
and indeed, there was movement.

01:27:46.720 --> 01:27:48.340
You mean, people like Brenda was teaching,

01:27:48.340 --> 01:27:52.730
there were, but it was most,
almost all at the junior ranks.

01:27:52.730 --> 01:27:56.920
There were efforts to
appoint more adjunct women,

01:27:56.920 --> 01:27:59.040
there are lots of faculty wives

01:27:59.040 --> 01:28:01.643
who had their PhD from this institution,

01:28:02.500 --> 01:28:07.500
but there was no concerted,
organized effort.

01:28:07.760 --> 01:28:11.080
Now there is, and it's a hard task,

01:28:11.080 --> 01:28:14.890
we've come a long way, we
have a long, long way to go.

01:28:14.890 --> 01:28:16.360
- What are the figures John?

01:28:16.360 --> 01:28:18.090
Do you know
- Pardon?

01:28:18.090 --> 01:28:19.280
- A full-time 10-year faculty
- No, I don't know

01:28:19.280 --> 01:28:21.993
Peter would know, I don't
know what the numbers are now.

01:28:21.993 --> 01:28:24.160
(mumbles)

01:28:25.880 --> 01:28:27.469
- So what is it now?

01:28:27.469 --> 01:28:28.860
(mumbles)

01:28:28.860 --> 01:28:30.190
- President Salovey is just saying

01:28:30.190 --> 01:28:32.790
every five years the ratio shifts,

01:28:32.790 --> 01:28:35.670
- You can really see the
demographics shifting.

01:28:35.670 --> 01:28:40.670
So we break down the gender
ratio in five year increments.

01:28:41.300 --> 01:28:42.880
And in the most recent years,

01:28:42.880 --> 01:28:46.850
the most recent hiring,
there's essentially parity

01:28:46.850 --> 01:28:48.830
in many many, many fields.

01:28:48.830 --> 01:28:52.900
But, you have a lot
of, what's a euphemism?

01:28:52.900 --> 01:28:57.830
Very senior faculty where the
ratios are very skewed still,

01:28:57.830 --> 01:29:00.980
and, but as they move toward retirement,

01:29:00.980 --> 01:29:02.680
you can see the replacement.

01:29:02.680 --> 01:29:04.280
I don't have the current number,

01:29:05.560 --> 01:29:07.640
Dean Chang is behind me and I can see he's

01:29:07.640 --> 01:29:09.241
on his cell phone looking it up.

01:29:09.241 --> 01:29:10.780
(audience laughs)

01:29:10.780 --> 01:29:12.030
Right now, maybe we'll have it

01:29:12.030 --> 01:29:14.278
before the session ends but

01:29:14.278 --> 01:29:16.690
(mumbles)

01:29:16.690 --> 01:29:20.454
- Yeah, that's what
he's looking at I think.

01:29:20.454 --> 01:29:21.420
- A (mumbles)

01:29:21.420 --> 01:29:24.260
- So 36%?
- Yes, yeah

01:29:24.260 --> 01:29:26.869
- 36% tenured or tenured track?

01:29:26.869 --> 01:29:27.702
- (mumbles)

01:29:27.702 --> 01:29:30.593
- In arts and sciences, right?

01:29:32.030 --> 01:29:33.430
- It seems--

01:29:33.430 --> 01:29:35.483
- Better but not there yet, yeah.

01:29:36.644 --> 01:29:39.390
- And, you know, that's a process

01:29:39.390 --> 01:29:41.760
that all hiring process is one that

01:29:43.480 --> 01:29:47.490
is really stuck in the 19th century

01:29:47.490 --> 01:29:51.723
because the faculty and
the faculty leadership,

01:29:52.580 --> 01:29:54.510
primarily responsible for it,

01:29:54.510 --> 01:29:57.220
the president doesn't just, hire,

01:29:57.220 --> 01:30:01.860
but I know during
president Levin's tenure,

01:30:01.860 --> 01:30:04.740
and I'm sure president
Salovey is continuing,

01:30:04.740 --> 01:30:07.600
there were actually incentives provided

01:30:07.600 --> 01:30:10.900
to the departments to go
out and try their best

01:30:10.900 --> 01:30:14.930
to get around the traditional pattern

01:30:14.930 --> 01:30:17.570
of looking for people who look like you,

01:30:17.570 --> 01:30:20.300
to succeed you on the faculty,

01:30:20.300 --> 01:30:23.220
and there is much more diversity

01:30:23.220 --> 01:30:26.260
because of those incentives
that were provided.

01:30:26.260 --> 01:30:30.700
But my goodness, it is
a very difficult process

01:30:30.700 --> 01:30:35.700
to get people to come out
of the traditional methods

01:30:35.700 --> 01:30:38.430
of selecting faculty and that has been one

01:30:38.430 --> 01:30:40.590
of the reasons it's been so slow,

01:30:40.590 --> 01:30:43.277
moving on the issue of women

01:30:43.277 --> 01:30:45.540
and as tenured faculty members.

01:30:45.540 --> 01:30:48.390
- Yeah, that's true, we do have incentives

01:30:48.390 --> 01:30:50.770
for hiring women, faculty and fields

01:30:50.770 --> 01:30:53.180
where women are underrepresented.

01:30:53.180 --> 01:30:55.233
I should also mention,

01:30:56.090 --> 01:30:59.943
Yale has 15 Deans running
professional schools,

01:31:01.430 --> 01:31:04.450
and the graduate school
and arts and sciences,

01:31:04.450 --> 01:31:07.390
and with the appointment of Nancy Brown

01:31:07.390 --> 01:31:10.780
as our new dean of the School of Medicine,

01:31:10.780 --> 01:31:13.010
she will start on February 1st,

01:31:13.010 --> 01:31:15.636
we now have eight out of 15

01:31:15.636 --> 01:31:17.178
of those Deans that are women so,

01:31:17.178 --> 01:31:19.928
(audience claps)

01:31:24.220 --> 01:31:26.650
- My name is Diana Wasserman
- (mumbles)

01:31:26.650 --> 01:31:31.540
- And I graduated from
medical school in 1977.

01:31:31.540 --> 01:31:34.053
I was admitted to the class of 73,

01:31:35.540 --> 01:31:38.200
but I'm speaking with another voice

01:31:39.370 --> 01:31:42.530
for somebody who isn't
here, which was my mother,

01:31:42.530 --> 01:31:44.153
Elga Wasserman.

01:31:44.153 --> 01:31:46.903
(audience claps)

01:32:02.270 --> 01:32:05.879
Thank you, she would be delighted.

01:32:05.879 --> 01:32:08.629
(audience claps)

01:32:13.440 --> 01:32:14.803
Thank you very much.

01:32:16.810 --> 01:32:18.720
I have a couple comments to make

01:32:19.900 --> 01:32:22.240
in anticipation of this weekend,

01:32:22.240 --> 01:32:27.240
I spent some time in her files

01:32:27.440 --> 01:32:32.440
and with my memories in the lead up

01:32:33.260 --> 01:32:37.770
to co-education, I was
a senior in high school

01:32:37.770 --> 01:32:40.883
and very much interested
in what was going on.

01:32:42.330 --> 01:32:47.030
I want to say, and this was stated

01:32:47.030 --> 01:32:51.170
in her oral histories, she got along well

01:32:51.170 --> 01:32:55.880
with Kingman Brewster, she respected him,

01:32:55.880 --> 01:32:57.510
and I think for the most part,

01:32:57.510 --> 01:33:02.510
he respected her, he didn't
know what he was getting,

01:33:02.720 --> 01:33:04.773
when he hired Elga Wasserman.

01:33:04.773 --> 01:33:05.800
(audience laughs)

01:33:05.800 --> 01:33:09.870
They spent a year or you
can correct me, John,

01:33:09.870 --> 01:33:13.010
a couple of years traveling
around the country,

01:33:13.010 --> 01:33:18.010
speaking to alumni groups,
and selling co-education.

01:33:18.580 --> 01:33:21.913
And, she had quite a few
stories to tell that year.

01:33:25.550 --> 01:33:30.550
She also had an office
over in Strathcona Hall

01:33:32.040 --> 01:33:34.930
where she did speak with many of you.

01:33:34.930 --> 01:33:37.670
And she spoke with
people who worked for her

01:33:37.670 --> 01:33:40.810
but also people who came with problems.

01:33:40.810 --> 01:33:45.810
And, I know that, and she recorded this

01:33:46.090 --> 01:33:50.397
that at one point, a student came to her

01:33:50.397 --> 01:33:54.053
and I don't know if that
person is here, saying,

01:33:56.137 --> 01:33:57.747
"I don't know what to do.

01:33:57.747 --> 01:34:01.737
"I spoke to my professor about starting

01:34:01.737 --> 01:34:04.777
"a women's studies program and he said,

01:34:04.777 --> 01:34:06.857
"Why would we want to do that?

01:34:06.857 --> 01:34:10.237
"We might as well start a program

01:34:10.237 --> 01:34:12.047
"on the history of dogs."

01:34:13.460 --> 01:34:17.077
And, that was, not common,

01:34:19.360 --> 01:34:22.180
but there were those elements.

01:34:22.180 --> 01:34:24.650
And there were elements at Yale,

01:34:24.650 --> 01:34:27.920
that did not want my mother to be a dean

01:34:27.920 --> 01:34:32.920
because it would demean
the position of Dean.

01:34:33.910 --> 01:34:38.910
My mother and father came
to New Haven in 1948.

01:34:38.970 --> 01:34:43.073
And for those of you who
were at Morse last night,

01:34:44.630 --> 01:34:49.630
this is a repetition, but my
dad got his PhD at Harvard,

01:34:51.120 --> 01:34:55.220
in the lab of a Nobel
laureate, and he came here,

01:34:55.220 --> 01:34:58.710
one of the first Jewish
professors at Yale,

01:34:58.710 --> 01:35:00.410
as an instructor.

01:35:00.410 --> 01:35:04.300
My mother got her PhD at Harvard

01:35:04.300 --> 01:35:07.420
in the same lab of a Nobel laureate.

01:35:07.420 --> 01:35:09.710
Although she said he was very invested

01:35:09.710 --> 01:35:12.670
in her career until she got married.

01:35:12.670 --> 01:35:17.520
And then he figured that she
wasn't serious about science.

01:35:17.520 --> 01:35:20.920
But, there were women around

01:35:20.920 --> 01:35:24.900
who could be hired on the Yale faculty.

01:35:24.900 --> 01:35:29.730
And, she, her efforts

01:35:30.600 --> 01:35:33.940
on behalf of faculty members

01:35:33.940 --> 01:35:38.940
and trying to get more faculty at Yale

01:35:39.000 --> 01:35:41.130
may have been why she didn't have

01:35:41.130 --> 01:35:43.890
a long term position
at Yale, I don't know,

01:35:43.890 --> 01:35:48.660
but I know that she felt
very strongly about that.

01:35:48.660 --> 01:35:51.300
She felt very strongly about the fact

01:35:51.300 --> 01:35:53.530
that there needed to be more women,

01:35:53.530 --> 01:35:57.340
and that the number of men
would have to be reduced

01:35:58.280 --> 01:36:01.130
or new colleges would have to be built.

01:36:01.130 --> 01:36:06.130
So, she was very fond of the
people she worked with at Yale,

01:36:07.230 --> 01:36:12.230
many of whom you're hearing
and will hear over the weekend.

01:36:13.320 --> 01:36:17.800
And, it was wonderful to
see John Wilkinson again,

01:36:17.800 --> 01:36:19.656
to see Sam Chauncey again.

01:36:19.656 --> 01:36:21.640
(audience claps)

01:36:21.640 --> 01:36:26.610
And to remember when I,
would come from school

01:36:26.610 --> 01:36:29.440
and meet her in her office,
there were always women

01:36:29.440 --> 01:36:33.660
in the office, they're all
always and in subsequent years,

01:36:33.660 --> 01:36:38.660
always students who felt that
that was a safe home for them.

01:36:39.190 --> 01:36:41.888
So, thank you for your greeting.

01:36:41.888 --> 01:36:44.638
(audience claps)

01:36:51.356 --> 01:36:53.350
- Thank you so much for adding this

01:36:53.350 --> 01:36:57.170
and I'm so glad I cut side of
you around the podium here.

01:36:57.170 --> 01:37:00.300
Congresswoman Jackson Lee
would like to say something,

01:37:00.300 --> 01:37:02.540
- For a moment I'm gonna
talk about bathroom break.

01:37:02.540 --> 01:37:04.124
But
- (mumbles) yeah, go ahead.

01:37:04.124 --> 01:37:06.470
(audience laughs)

01:37:06.470 --> 01:37:08.730
- But let me add my appreciation

01:37:08.730 --> 01:37:13.730
to Elsa's beautiful daughter
and well, well spoken.

01:37:14.600 --> 01:37:18.950
She is our (mumbles) and
we thank you so very much.

01:37:18.950 --> 01:37:22.690
I like experiments, I
like being thrown into

01:37:22.690 --> 01:37:27.660
a boiling pot and survive
it, of either hot water

01:37:27.660 --> 01:37:30.290
or cold water but let
me rise particularly.

01:37:30.290 --> 01:37:34.060
I will speak this afternoon
to express my appreciation

01:37:34.060 --> 01:37:39.060
for the panel, for my
sister's fellow travelers

01:37:39.080 --> 01:37:43.020
and Kurt schmoke, my fellow,

01:37:43.020 --> 01:37:48.020
shut the Yale campus down
component but keep it.

01:37:49.320 --> 01:37:52.530
Garry, thank you for your continued humor

01:37:53.524 --> 01:37:56.840
and political satire
that has made those of us

01:37:56.840 --> 01:37:59.510
in this life know what reality is.

01:37:59.510 --> 01:38:01.670
And John
(audience laughs)

01:38:01.670 --> 01:38:04.280
John you were a lifeline,
we appreciate it very much.

01:38:04.280 --> 01:38:06.568
I'm just gonna make this,
we really do appreciate it.

01:38:06.568 --> 01:38:08.670
(audience laughs)

01:38:08.670 --> 01:38:10.530
President and officers, what I would like

01:38:10.530 --> 01:38:13.080
to say as a woman of
color, and to thank you

01:38:13.080 --> 01:38:16.780
to all of the women who
had the brilliant idea

01:38:16.780 --> 01:38:19.960
to bring us together, so
the bus driver could say

01:38:19.960 --> 01:38:22.350
that I didn't look like I
had been here 50 years ago,

01:38:22.350 --> 01:38:23.545
that was pretty good,

01:38:23.545 --> 01:38:26.940
(audience laughs)
(audience claps)

01:38:26.940 --> 01:38:29.720
Let me clearly say in the toxicity

01:38:29.720 --> 01:38:34.720
of which I live in, in this
world of public service,

01:38:35.350 --> 01:38:40.350
and this world of attempting
to reach America's Promise,

01:38:40.900 --> 01:38:45.900
I valued the welcoming
with its hills and valleys,

01:38:47.800 --> 01:38:51.760
that Yale had in diversity,
in the midst of all of us,

01:38:51.760 --> 01:38:54.340
was the beginning of
the cultural diversity

01:38:54.340 --> 01:38:56.850
and the houses that now exist,

01:38:56.850 --> 01:38:58.400
that those of us who are (mumbles)

01:38:58.400 --> 01:39:01.230
who care about the African American house

01:39:01.230 --> 01:39:04.470
and those who are Latino,
those who are Asian,

01:39:04.470 --> 01:39:07.740
those who are a variety of advocacies.

01:39:07.740 --> 01:39:09.510
So in the midst of all of that,

01:39:09.510 --> 01:39:11.720
we should realize as women, there were

01:39:11.720 --> 01:39:16.720
this cultural, outside
world from the Vietnam War,

01:39:17.340 --> 01:39:20.070
to trials that were right down from us,

01:39:20.070 --> 01:39:24.483
those of us who were here in
that summer or spring of 70,

01:39:25.540 --> 01:39:27.660
and a Kingman Brewster who came over

01:39:27.660 --> 01:39:30.610
on pilgrims (mumbles)
meeting his ancestors,

01:39:30.610 --> 01:39:35.540
I came in a different
boat, was able to keep

01:39:35.540 --> 01:39:39.060
the stability while this experiment

01:39:39.060 --> 01:39:42.720
and while we were trying
to find bathrooms,

01:39:42.720 --> 01:39:46.700
while we were in class, and
I, my children enjoy me saying

01:39:46.700 --> 01:39:50.570
that I had to run to my
dorm to use the restroom,

01:39:50.570 --> 01:39:52.620
think that's all right to be able to say,

01:39:53.810 --> 01:39:55.590
but the experiment is what I hope

01:39:55.590 --> 01:39:57.883
as we proceed through this weekend,

01:39:58.890 --> 01:40:03.420
that we can reflect on, and
even maybe leave this place

01:40:03.420 --> 01:40:06.680
as a person who deals with
criminal justice reform,

01:40:06.680 --> 01:40:09.330
you have touched my conscious

01:40:09.330 --> 01:40:13.250
and, so I'm running back
now with a whole new idea

01:40:13.250 --> 01:40:16.270
about dealing with the
talent that we tried

01:40:16.270 --> 01:40:17.800
to say get back into the community,

01:40:17.800 --> 01:40:19.390
I hope we all have a chance to have

01:40:19.390 --> 01:40:21.370
a conversation, I'm
delighted with your work.

01:40:21.370 --> 01:40:23.040
But the point is, I just wanna end

01:40:23.040 --> 01:40:26.423
on the note of appreciation
of this whole weekend.

01:40:27.660 --> 01:40:30.110
I have some trials and
tribulations in the Congress,

01:40:30.110 --> 01:40:32.520
I have some trials and
tribulations in my own district.

01:40:32.520 --> 01:40:34.590
But I thought it was important enough

01:40:34.590 --> 01:40:38.430
to be able to come to get
a better understanding

01:40:38.430 --> 01:40:42.290
of how we can utilize
this experiment even now,

01:40:42.290 --> 01:40:45.423
some five decades later,

01:40:46.420 --> 01:40:49.650
and saying that diversity works,

01:40:49.650 --> 01:40:52.230
that empowering women works,

01:40:52.230 --> 01:40:54.430
that our ideas are important,

01:40:54.430 --> 01:40:59.250
and even today, in 2019,
let it be very clear

01:40:59.250 --> 01:41:01.910
as we advocate for the
Equal Rights Amendment

01:41:01.910 --> 01:41:04.450
in the United States
Congress and the America,

01:41:04.450 --> 01:41:07.440
United States of America,
we haven't passed it yet.

01:41:07.440 --> 01:41:10.370
So, I wanna thank Yale for being willing

01:41:10.370 --> 01:41:13.070
to be in the fight, because
you're still in the fight.

01:41:13.960 --> 01:41:17.210
And I wanna thank you for allowing

01:41:17.210 --> 01:41:21.473
the few women of color that
came in that fall of 1969,

01:41:22.680 --> 01:41:26.830
to have voices to experience
things that were unspeakable,

01:41:26.830 --> 01:41:30.573
some of us will talk about,
to experience discrimination,

01:41:31.750 --> 01:41:35.010
to experience embracing, to find sisters

01:41:35.010 --> 01:41:38.740
that we had never interacted with before,

01:41:38.740 --> 01:41:41.930
but to be able to attack
this thing called diversity,

01:41:41.930 --> 01:41:43.960
and to realize that America's Promise,

01:41:43.960 --> 01:41:46.530
not yet finished, was, we were part of it,

01:41:46.530 --> 01:41:48.680
and what Yale was hoping to do,

01:41:48.680 --> 01:41:50.500
and I was hoping that they continue to do,

01:41:50.500 --> 01:41:53.600
is to stay part of the
fight for America's Promise.

01:41:53.600 --> 01:41:56.930
I went on too long, I know
that you need a bathroom break,

01:41:56.930 --> 01:41:58.850
but I'm delighted to see all of you.

01:41:58.850 --> 01:42:00.320
I'm trying to recognize (mumbles)

01:42:00.320 --> 01:42:03.180
but you all look glamorous, fabulous.

01:42:03.180 --> 01:42:05.181
Thank you very much, thank you all.

01:42:05.181 --> 01:42:07.931
(audience claps)

